So congratulations.
Thank you.

58
00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:44.590
It's an amazing work.

59
00:02:44.590 --> 00:02:48.230
And I've been following your trajectory for a while.

60
00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:51.060
And the impact that you're having on culture

61
00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:53.060
is really quite something, it's profound.

62
00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:54.810
And so thank you for the work that you do,

63
00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:58.000
before we even get into it.
I appreciate it.

64
00:02:58.000 --> 00:02:59.870
In looking, you know,

65
00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:03.890
sort of to introduce you to the audience,

66
00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:06.520
you're now in your third book, the first book "Sapiens"

67
00:03:06.520 --> 00:03:09.607
cast a glance backwards on the history of humanity,

68
00:03:09.607 --> 00:03:12.830
"Homo Deus" cast that glance forward

69
00:03:12.830 --> 00:03:16.240
at what we can anticipate in the future years to come.

70
00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:20.210
And this newest book roots us in the presence.

71
00:03:20.210 --> 00:03:23.790
We're in very interesting times right now, confusing times,

72
00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:26.610
you're trying to make sense of what is going on,

73
00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:28.930
and trying to cut a path forward

74
00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:30.830
in the interest of humanity.

75
00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:35.593
And I think, you know, to kind of launch into this,

76
00:03:37.010 --> 00:03:41.670
the first sentence in the book really sets the tone,

77
00:03:41.670 --> 00:03:43.900
and kind of encapsulates everything that you're about,

78
00:03:43.900 --> 00:03:47.220
and the work that you do, which is, clarity is power.

79
00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:50.310
So can you explain that concept for me?

80
00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:51.240
Yes.

81
00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:54.050
It's common to say that information is power,

82
00:03:54.050 --> 00:03:55.640
and knowledge is power.

83
00:03:55.640 --> 00:03:57.773
And this was true for much of history,

84
00:03:57.773 --> 00:04:00.340
when information was very scarce,

85
00:04:00.340 --> 00:04:04.480
and the censorship worked by withholding information,

86
00:04:04.480 --> 00:04:07.300
by blocking the flow of information.

87
00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:09.570
But we now live in a very different age

88
00:04:09.570 --> 00:04:13.420
when we are flooded by enormous amounts of information,

89
00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:14.750
we have far too much of it,

90
00:04:14.750 --> 00:04:16.830
we don't know how to make sense of it.

91
00:04:16.830 --> 00:04:21.710
And censorship now actually works by distracting people

92
00:04:21.710 --> 00:04:24.500
with too much information, with irrelevant information,

93
00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:26.270
with disinformation.

94
00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:31.270
And in this age, clarity is more important than ever before,

95
00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:35.000
because we need to know what to focus on.

96
00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:39.590
Attention becomes maybe the most scarce resource of all,

97
00:04:39.590 --> 00:04:42.680
and where to direct your attention,

98
00:04:42.680 --> 00:04:46.263
and how to keep your attention on the important things,

99
00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:49.540
this is extremely important.

100
00:04:49.540 --> 00:04:50.890
And one of the things that,

101
00:04:50.890 --> 00:04:55.040
I guess, differentiate the powerful people today,

102
00:04:55.040 --> 00:04:58.020
from the less powerful is knowing

103
00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:00.680
what to do with their attention.

104
00:05:00.680 --> 00:05:03.020
Yeah, there's never been greater demands

105
00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:04.480
for our attention.

106
00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:05.860
You know, things like fake news.

107
00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:07.590
Although, you know, ascending,

108
00:05:07.590 --> 00:05:09.350
and we're having this fake news moment,

109
00:05:09.350 --> 00:05:10.970
are certainly nothing new.
No.

110
00:05:10.970 --> 00:05:12.800
Misinformation and disinformation

111
00:05:12.800 --> 00:05:15.640
and propaganda are as old as humanity,

112
00:05:15.640 --> 00:05:19.770
but this watershed moment that we're having,

113
00:05:19.770 --> 00:05:22.750
where we're just inundated with attention

114
00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:25.670
by virtue of devices that are specifically designed

115
00:05:25.670 --> 00:05:28.773
to addict us and distract us,

116
00:05:29.757 --> 00:05:33.210
it's become increasingly more and more difficult

117
00:05:33.210 --> 00:05:38.210
to discern truth from fiction, reality from obfuscation.

118
00:05:38.330 --> 00:05:43.330
And to just see to kind of navigate our lives

119
00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:46.500
with that sense of clarity.

120
00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:49.620
Yeah. And I would agree that fake news

121
00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:52.980
and disinformation are definitely not something new.

122
00:05:52.980 --> 00:05:55.880
We had them from the very beginning of history,

123
00:05:55.880 --> 00:05:59.530
and in many ways it was much worse in the past.

124
00:05:59.530 --> 00:06:01.850
With all the talk about, you know, Facebook and Twitter,

125
00:06:01.850 --> 00:06:04.330
spreading rumors and fake news.

126
00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:07.030
Just think about yourself in some medieval village

127
00:06:07.030 --> 00:06:10.370
1,000 years ago, maybe in England somewhere,

128
00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:12.867
and somebody comes along and tells you,

129
00:06:12.867 --> 00:06:15.220
"Hey, do you know this old lady

130
00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:17.340
who lives at the edge of the village?

131
00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:20.280
I just saw her flying on a broom stick."

132
00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:22.620
And within an hour,

133
00:06:22.620 --> 00:06:27.060
you would have a raging mob with pitchforks and torches,

134
00:06:30.400 --> 00:06:32.863
ready to burn this old lady to death.

135
00:06:33.980 --> 00:06:38.980
So fake news is not a new problem created by Facebook

136
00:06:39.290 --> 00:06:42.884
or Twitter or Putin or Trump or anything like that.

137
00:06:42.884 --> 00:06:47.884
What is new is that now we are surrounded

138
00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:55.620
by devices which were designed to hack our brains

139
00:06:57.360 --> 00:07:00.330
in a way which was never possible before,

140
00:07:00.330 --> 00:07:01.600
because until today,

141
00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:03.713
nobody really understood the human brain.

142
00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:06.160
In the middle ages,

143
00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:08.970
their understanding of how the human body

144
00:07:08.970 --> 00:07:10.700
and how the human brain function,

145
00:07:10.700 --> 00:07:12.920
and how human attention functions

146
00:07:12.920 --> 00:07:14.940
was extremely integumentary.

147
00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:17.100
So yes, people knew a few tricks

148
00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:19.060
about how to grab attention,

149
00:07:19.060 --> 00:07:22.100
but it was nothing like what we see today,

150
00:07:22.100 --> 00:07:26.660
simply because science has progressed since the middle ages.

151
00:07:26.660 --> 00:07:30.680
And I think maybe the most important thing for people

152
00:07:30.680 --> 00:07:34.010
to realize about living in the 21st century,

153
00:07:34.010 --> 00:07:37.500
as against middle ages or the stone age,

154
00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:40.890
is that we are now hackable animals.

155
00:07:40.890 --> 00:07:43.020
People don't want to hear that.

156
00:07:43.020 --> 00:07:44.180
I know.
We like to think

157
00:07:44.180 --> 00:07:47.790
that we're sentient and that we have agency, you know,

158
00:07:47.790 --> 00:07:51.730
beyond the capabilities that you're sort of writing about.

159
00:07:51.730 --> 00:07:53.070
Yes, we are sentient.

160
00:07:53.070 --> 00:07:56.850
We do have agency, but something is changing.

161
00:07:56.850 --> 00:08:01.840
Until today, basically, nobody could really hack us.

162
00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:03.730
We're just too complicated.

163
00:08:03.730 --> 00:08:06.860
And science and technology were too primitive.

164
00:08:06.860 --> 00:08:09.047
So if you went around believing that,

165
00:08:09.047 --> 00:08:10.930
"Hey, I'm a free agent,

166
00:08:10.930 --> 00:08:13.700
nobody can really look into my brain,

167
00:08:13.700 --> 00:08:16.130
nobody can really understand my mind,

168
00:08:16.130 --> 00:08:18.900
nobody can manipulate me and predict

169
00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:20.180
what I'm going to do next."

170
00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:24.680
This was true, but this is no longer true.

171
00:08:24.680 --> 00:08:28.570
Humans are extremely complicated animals,

172
00:08:28.570 --> 00:08:31.440
but they are not infinitely complicated.

173
00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:36.303
And in order for us to cross this watershed,

174
00:08:37.290 --> 00:08:42.290
we don't really need algorithms that know us perfectly.

175
00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:44.500
This is impossible.

176
00:08:44.500 --> 00:08:46.450
Nothing is perfect in the world.

177
00:08:46.450 --> 00:08:49.550
You can't build a system that predicts everything,

178
00:08:49.550 --> 00:08:51.620
or that understands you perfectly,

179
00:08:51.620 --> 00:08:53.690
but in order to have a big revolution

180
00:08:53.690 --> 00:08:57.690
in politics, in culture, you don't need a perfect system.

181
00:08:57.690 --> 00:09:02.180
The system just needs to be better than the average human.

182
00:09:02.180 --> 00:09:04.560
And this is not so very difficult,

183
00:09:04.560 --> 00:09:09.560
because humans often make terrible mistakes in the decisions

184
00:09:09.790 --> 00:09:12.040
and the most important decisions of their lives.

185
00:09:12.040 --> 00:09:13.910
Humans often know quite...

186
00:09:13.910 --> 00:09:16.390
Surprisingly little about themselves,

187
00:09:16.390 --> 00:09:19.560
about their desires, about their minds.

188
00:09:19.560 --> 00:09:23.720
So if this is the yardstick to build an algorithm

189
00:09:23.720 --> 00:09:27.360
that knows you better than you know yourself,

190
00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:29.270
this is not an impossible mission.

191
00:09:29.270 --> 00:09:34.270
And we are very close to the point when somebody like Amazon

192
00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:36.210
or like the Chinese government

193
00:09:36.210 --> 00:09:39.280
are going to have these kinds of algorithms,

194
00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:40.250
these kinds of systems.

195
00:09:40.250 --> 00:09:42.220
Yeah, what's interesting about it is that,

196
00:09:42.220 --> 00:09:44.810
it's not happening against our will.

197
00:09:44.810 --> 00:09:47.660
We're voluntarily signing up for this.

198
00:09:47.660 --> 00:09:50.740
And I think most people are proceeding on this assumption

199
00:09:50.740 --> 00:09:53.247
that these tools are making our lives better,

200
00:09:53.247 --> 00:09:55.270
"What's wrong with Amazon predicting

201
00:09:55.270 --> 00:09:57.100
the books that I wanna read?

202
00:09:57.100 --> 00:09:59.510
What's wrong with me being delivered

203
00:09:59.510 --> 00:10:02.410
that kind of advertising of the products

204
00:10:02.410 --> 00:10:04.380
that I already wanna buy?"

205
00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:06.270
It seems...

206
00:10:06.270 --> 00:10:08.733
You know, if not benevolent,

207
00:10:10.611 --> 00:10:13.010
on some level not malevolent.
Yeah.

208
00:10:13.010 --> 00:10:15.680
I mean, first of all, this is part of the design.

209
00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:19.180
I mean, this is not the secret police

210
00:10:19.180 --> 00:10:21.760
from George Orwell in 1984.

211
00:10:21.760 --> 00:10:24.900
This is the secret police from "Brave New World."

212
00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:28.060
I mean, it works by understanding you,

213
00:10:28.060 --> 00:10:31.940
and by appealing to your own cravings, your own emotions.

214
00:10:31.940 --> 00:10:36.260
So yes, I mean, the system works by making you feel

215
00:10:36.260 --> 00:10:38.200
that they're on your side.
Right.

216
00:10:38.200 --> 00:10:40.500
Otherwise, it wouldn't be this kind of system,

217
00:10:40.500 --> 00:10:43.790
it will be the old style, the Gestapo or KGB.

218
00:10:43.790 --> 00:10:48.790
But equally, and even more importantly,

219
00:10:49.030 --> 00:10:51.540
in many cases, it is benevolent.

220
00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:53.430
I mean, depicting it as some kind

221
00:10:53.430 --> 00:10:56.550
of terrible conspiracy misses the point.

222
00:10:56.550 --> 00:10:57.670
If everything...

223
00:10:57.670 --> 00:11:00.870
If all these developments were just a terrible conspiracy

224
00:11:00.870 --> 00:11:04.220
to abuse us, then I don't think

225
00:11:04.220 --> 00:11:06.050
it would've worked very well.

226
00:11:06.050 --> 00:11:09.120
The point is that in many cases,

227
00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:11.900
these systems do understand us better,

228
00:11:11.900 --> 00:11:14.520
and can improve our lives in many ways.

229
00:11:14.520 --> 00:11:16.930
And this is the temptation.

230
00:11:16.930 --> 00:11:19.980
I mean, in some cases it's obvious,

231
00:11:19.980 --> 00:11:23.880
if you think about a healthcare system

232
00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:26.460
that monitors what's happening inside your body.

233
00:11:26.460 --> 00:11:29.330
And yes, it knows what's happening inside your body

234
00:11:29.330 --> 00:11:32.690
better than your conscious mind.

235
00:11:32.690 --> 00:11:35.590
If you have, I dunno, cancer spreading in your body,

236
00:11:35.590 --> 00:11:39.420
very often, people become aware of it only

237
00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:42.830
when it's already a big problem.

238
00:11:42.830 --> 00:11:45.510
And you start feeling pain and you don't know what it is.

239
00:11:45.510 --> 00:11:48.160
So you go to the doctor and you have this exam

240
00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:49.877
and this test, and then they find,

241
00:11:49.877 --> 00:11:53.170
"Oh, you have cancer spreading in your liver or something."

242
00:11:53.170 --> 00:11:56.981
And it's now going to be very difficult, very painful,

243
00:11:56.981 --> 00:12:00.830
very expensive to deal with it.

244
00:12:00.830 --> 00:12:03.800
And the alternative is to have the system

245
00:12:03.800 --> 00:12:07.860
that constantly monitors what's happening in your body,

246
00:12:07.860 --> 00:12:12.210
and is able to discover that a cancer

247
00:12:12.210 --> 00:12:15.110
is starting to spread in your liver

248
00:12:15.110 --> 00:12:16.810
when it's still in the initial stages,

249
00:12:16.810 --> 00:12:21.810
you don't feel anything, but the biometric sensors,

250
00:12:22.340 --> 00:12:27.290
they are already picking up the first telltale signs

251
00:12:27.290 --> 00:12:30.230
that cancer is beginning when it's still very easy,

252
00:12:30.230 --> 00:12:33.290
very cheap, pain free, to get rid of it.

253
00:12:33.290 --> 00:12:34.730
So this is wonderful.

254
00:12:34.730 --> 00:12:36.320
Why would we like...

255
00:12:36.320 --> 00:12:39.120
Why would we want to block this kind of development?

256
00:12:39.120 --> 00:12:42.720
And it's also the case that in many decisions in life,

257
00:12:42.720 --> 00:12:47.720
from which movie to watch, to what to study in university,

258
00:12:48.040 --> 00:12:52.460
we could use help, or we have to make very bad choices.

259
00:12:52.460 --> 00:12:54.030
So if that would've helped me out a lot,

260
00:12:54.030 --> 00:12:55.612
when I was younger.
Yeah.

261
00:12:55.612 --> 00:12:57.620
It would save me a decade.

262
00:12:57.620 --> 00:12:59.160
So the whole problem

263
00:12:59.160 --> 00:13:03.940
is that there is a huge temptation there.

264
00:13:03.940 --> 00:13:08.870
It's not just a kind of malevolent conspiracy.

265
00:13:08.870 --> 00:13:11.260
There are downsides,

266
00:13:11.260 --> 00:13:16.000
but how to resist the temptations

267
00:13:16.000 --> 00:13:18.820
or how to enjoy the benefits,

268
00:13:18.820 --> 00:13:22.240
without suffering the harmful consequences,

269
00:13:22.240 --> 00:13:24.080
this is the big challenge.

270
00:13:24.080 --> 00:13:27.170
And just saying, "Oh, this is all a terrible development.

271
00:13:27.170 --> 00:13:29.710
We should just unplug ourself completely

272
00:13:29.710 --> 00:13:32.940
from all the devices," this is not going to work.

273
00:13:32.940 --> 00:13:35.180
And this is probably a bad idea,

274
00:13:35.180 --> 00:13:39.150
because we would be missing so many positive developments.

275
00:13:39.150 --> 00:13:41.140
Right, I'm wearing a ring right now

276
00:13:41.140 --> 00:13:43.220
that is actually a biometric device

277
00:13:43.220 --> 00:13:44.810
that measures my heart rate

278
00:13:44.810 --> 00:13:47.700
and my heart rate variability and my various sleep states.

279
00:13:47.700 --> 00:13:50.663
And I'm looking at this as a tool, as a benefit,

280
00:13:51.790 --> 00:13:56.730
sort of, blisfully divorcing myself from the fact

281
00:13:56.730 --> 00:14:00.250
that I am a product of big data.

282
00:14:00.250 --> 00:14:02.080
And right now we're in this moment

283
00:14:02.080 --> 00:14:05.610
of convergence of infotech and biotech

284
00:14:05.610 --> 00:14:08.610
that's gonna reshape how we live

285
00:14:08.610 --> 00:14:10.790
and what our world is gonna look like.

286
00:14:10.790 --> 00:14:13.323
So play this dystopia out for me.

287
00:14:14.600 --> 00:14:15.443
Oh, well, if you want to go

288
00:14:15.443 --> 00:14:19.320
into dystopian direction, then...

289
00:14:19.320 --> 00:14:22.140
You know, the question is, "Who knows...

290
00:14:22.140 --> 00:14:25.600
Who gets the information that this biometric device

291
00:14:25.600 --> 00:14:30.600
is collecting on you and what are they going to do with it?"

292
00:14:31.490 --> 00:14:36.490
And if this kind of information is being harvested

293
00:14:36.510 --> 00:14:39.530
by some big corporation or by some government,

294
00:14:39.530 --> 00:14:43.090
and you have no idea what they are doing,

295
00:14:43.090 --> 00:14:46.973
then this could lead in all kinds of dystopian directions.

296
00:14:48.010 --> 00:14:52.280
One dystopian scenario, just imagine North Korea,

297
00:14:52.280 --> 00:14:55.340
having these rings mass produced,

298
00:14:55.340 --> 00:14:59.890
and forcing every citizen of North Korea

299
00:14:59.890 --> 00:15:02.800
to go around 24 hours with this ring,

300
00:15:02.800 --> 00:15:06.530
and to give all the information to some central database.

301
00:15:06.530 --> 00:15:09.370
And you walk into a room and you look at a picture

302
00:15:09.370 --> 00:15:10.880
of Kim Jong-un on the wall,

303
00:15:10.880 --> 00:15:14.610
and the ring picks up the signs of anger.

304
00:15:14.610 --> 00:15:15.621
Right.

305
00:15:15.621 --> 00:15:16.454
Or the signs of dissatisfaction.

306
00:15:16.454 --> 00:15:17.670
Next stop, the Gulag.

307
00:15:17.670 --> 00:15:19.170
Yeah, next stop is the Gulag.

308
00:15:19.170 --> 00:15:21.160
It's almost like minority report,

309
00:15:21.160 --> 00:15:24.850
like a precognition model of trying to figure out,

310
00:15:24.850 --> 00:15:27.462
who's gonna be an enemy of the state

311
00:15:27.462 --> 00:15:30.170
before an act is even perpetrated.

312
00:15:30.170 --> 00:15:31.980
Not only before the act is perpetrated,

313
00:15:31.980 --> 00:15:35.370
before you even think about doing something.

314
00:15:35.370 --> 00:15:36.910
I mean, you can pick...

315
00:15:36.910 --> 00:15:38.594
If you follow all the...

316
00:15:38.594 --> 00:15:40.640
I don't know how many citizens there are in North Korea,

317
00:15:40.640 --> 00:15:42.360
like 20 million or 30 million.

318
00:15:42.360 --> 00:15:45.320
If you follow enough of them for enough time,

319
00:15:45.320 --> 00:15:47.620
you can build the profile,

320
00:15:47.620 --> 00:15:50.560
or that you can pick up the dissidents

321
00:15:50.560 --> 00:15:52.263
when they are in kindergarten.

322
00:15:53.370 --> 00:15:58.180
You don't need to wait until they are 30 years old.

323
00:15:58.180 --> 00:16:02.840
And working in the department of transport in Pyongyang,

324
00:16:02.840 --> 00:16:04.920
and having these schemes to do...

325
00:16:04.920 --> 00:16:07.450
No, you can pick them up in kindergarten

326
00:16:07.450 --> 00:16:10.290
and re-educate them or whatever.

327
00:16:10.290 --> 00:16:13.590
So if you want to go dystopian, there are...

328
00:16:13.590 --> 00:16:16.760
Many just think what Stalin would've would've done

329
00:16:16.760 --> 00:16:20.933
if he had these kinds of biometric technologies.

330
00:16:22.000 --> 00:16:26.120
Yeah. We tend to think of this age of technology

331
00:16:26.120 --> 00:16:27.860
as being in its adolescence.

332
00:16:27.860 --> 00:16:31.158
But I think we're really in its infancy.

333
00:16:31.158 --> 00:16:31.991
Yes.

334
00:16:31.991 --> 00:16:32.880
We're still in the birth canal here.

335
00:16:32.880 --> 00:16:36.130
And we have no idea how to telescope forward

336
00:16:36.130 --> 00:16:38.270
into what this is gonna look like.

337
00:16:38.270 --> 00:16:42.830
And you, with your superpowers of clarity,

338
00:16:42.830 --> 00:16:46.400
have given us a glimpse of what perhaps is to come.

339
00:16:46.400 --> 00:16:49.700
And the argument that you make is that,

340
00:16:49.700 --> 00:16:52.450
big data hearkens the end of humanism.

341
00:16:52.450 --> 00:16:54.320
So can you explain what humanism is

342
00:16:54.320 --> 00:16:56.490
and what you mean by that?

343
00:16:56.490 --> 00:16:58.440
Well, humanism is the belief

344
00:16:58.440 --> 00:17:00.650
in the supremacy of humanity,

345
00:17:00.650 --> 00:17:03.460
and in particular, human feelings.

346
00:17:03.460 --> 00:17:07.030
You can kind of encapsulate humanism in the idea,

347
00:17:07.030 --> 00:17:11.140
that human feelings are the ultimate authority

348
00:17:11.140 --> 00:17:14.070
in the universe, or at least on earth.

349
00:17:14.070 --> 00:17:18.280
And in politics, this means that the voter knows best.

350
00:17:18.280 --> 00:17:20.230
The highest authority in politics

351
00:17:20.230 --> 00:17:23.220
is the feelings of the voters.

352
00:17:23.220 --> 00:17:26.740
And should be very clear that we are talking about feelings,

353
00:17:26.740 --> 00:17:28.910
not about rationality.

354
00:17:28.910 --> 00:17:33.410
When you go, you have a referendum, or you have an election,

355
00:17:33.410 --> 00:17:36.340
you are appealing to the feelings of the voters,

356
00:17:36.340 --> 00:17:38.300
not to their rationality.

357
00:17:38.300 --> 00:17:41.070
If elections were about rationality,

358
00:17:41.070 --> 00:17:42.590
there was absolutely no reason

359
00:17:42.590 --> 00:17:44.930
to give equal voting rights to everybody.

360
00:17:44.930 --> 00:17:47.480
We know perfectly well that different people

361
00:17:47.480 --> 00:17:50.340
have different irrational capabilities

362
00:17:50.340 --> 00:17:54.250
and different knowledge, expertise, and so forth.

363
00:17:54.250 --> 00:17:56.410
When you need to...

364
00:17:56.410 --> 00:17:59.040
Again, if you have cancer and you go to the hospital,

365
00:17:59.040 --> 00:18:00.380
you go to the expert.

366
00:18:00.380 --> 00:18:02.420
You don't have a referendum

367
00:18:02.420 --> 00:18:04.510
between all the people you meet on the street.

368
00:18:04.510 --> 00:18:08.070
You want the person who has the best capabilities,

369
00:18:08.070 --> 00:18:10.610
but in humanist politics, no,

370
00:18:10.610 --> 00:18:13.080
you appeal to the feelings of individuals.

371
00:18:13.080 --> 00:18:14.593
This is the highest authority.

372
00:18:15.560 --> 00:18:19.420
In the field of economics, it manifests itself in the idea

373
00:18:19.420 --> 00:18:21.880
that the customer is always right.

374
00:18:21.880 --> 00:18:25.010
The highest authority in a humanist economy

375
00:18:25.010 --> 00:18:27.160
is the feelings of the customers.

376
00:18:27.160 --> 00:18:29.600
If the customers want something...

377
00:18:29.600 --> 00:18:33.100
And it doesn't matter why, because it's a crazy whim.

378
00:18:33.100 --> 00:18:36.020
It's a fad, it's a fashion, who cares?

379
00:18:36.020 --> 00:18:38.780
This is the highest authority in the economy.

380
00:18:38.780 --> 00:18:41.250
If everybody wants it, or if enough people want it,

381
00:18:41.250 --> 00:18:42.880
they vote with their credit cards,

382
00:18:42.880 --> 00:18:43.897
there is nobody who can say,

383
00:18:43.897 --> 00:18:45.960
"Well, but this is bad for you."

384
00:18:45.960 --> 00:18:47.043
Who are you?

385
00:18:47.043 --> 00:18:50.570
I mean, my feelings are the highest authority.

386
00:18:50.570 --> 00:18:51.950
And it's the same in art.

387
00:18:51.950 --> 00:18:54.230
In art humanism...

388
00:18:54.230 --> 00:18:57.497
The humanist slogan in art is,

389
00:18:57.497 --> 00:19:00.070
"Beauty is in the eyes of the beholder."

390
00:19:00.070 --> 00:19:02.410
It's just like, the customer is always right.

391
00:19:02.410 --> 00:19:06.595
There is no authority higher than the beholder.

392
00:19:06.595 --> 00:19:11.433
And if you like a certain genre or a certain piece of art,

393
00:19:12.350 --> 00:19:15.050
your feelings are the ultimate judge.

394
00:19:15.050 --> 00:19:16.387
There is nobody out there who can tell you,

395
00:19:16.387 --> 00:19:20.310
"Yes, you like it, but it's actually bad, actually bad art."

396
00:19:20.310 --> 00:19:24.810
And in ethics, the idea is, just follow your heart.

397
00:19:24.810 --> 00:19:26.330
Just do what feels good.

398
00:19:26.330 --> 00:19:28.120
If it feels good, do it.

399
00:19:28.120 --> 00:19:30.040
If it doesn't feel good, don't do it.

400
00:19:30.040 --> 00:19:32.580
Of course, sometimes your good feelings collide

401
00:19:32.580 --> 00:19:34.087
with somebody else's bad feelings,

402
00:19:34.087 --> 00:19:36.610
"I feel very good about stealing your car,

403
00:19:36.610 --> 00:19:38.130
you feel very bad about it."

404
00:19:38.130 --> 00:19:40.063
So we now have an ethical debate.

405
00:19:41.630 --> 00:19:43.240
And when you look today in the world,

406
00:19:43.240 --> 00:19:47.160
you see very complicated ethical debates

407
00:19:47.160 --> 00:19:49.700
around things like identity politics,

408
00:19:49.700 --> 00:19:53.450
which actually revolve around human feelings.

409
00:19:53.450 --> 00:19:54.590
This is the highest authority.

410
00:19:54.590 --> 00:19:57.410
You don't appeal to God or to the Bible or to the Pope,

411
00:19:57.410 --> 00:19:59.720
you appeal to, "I feel."

412
00:19:59.720 --> 00:20:03.010
And all this comes also down to education.

413
00:20:03.010 --> 00:20:06.410
The highest ideal of education, according to humanism,

414
00:20:06.410 --> 00:20:09.420
is to teach people to think for themselves.

415
00:20:09.420 --> 00:20:11.610
Again, they are the highest authority.

416
00:20:11.610 --> 00:20:13.070
So this is humanism like...

417
00:20:13.070 --> 00:20:14.890
Right, that economic forces,

418
00:20:14.890 --> 00:20:17.440
political forces, cultural forces,

419
00:20:17.440 --> 00:20:21.540
all bend to the will and the whims of the collective

420
00:20:22.520 --> 00:20:25.130
emotional landscape of the population.

421
00:20:25.130 --> 00:20:25.963
Exactly.

422
00:20:25.963 --> 00:20:28.400
And the only thing that can counter

423
00:20:28.400 --> 00:20:31.370
the feelings of a human being is the feelings

424
00:20:31.370 --> 00:20:34.883
of another human being, that's the game.

425
00:20:35.790 --> 00:20:40.210
And all this is now threatened by what happens

426
00:20:40.210 --> 00:20:45.150
when a big data algorithm can hack the human system,

427
00:20:45.150 --> 00:20:47.950
and cannot only predict people's feelings,

428
00:20:47.950 --> 00:20:49.210
so you don't need to ask them,

429
00:20:49.210 --> 00:20:51.490
you know better than they, what they feel.

430
00:20:51.490 --> 00:20:53.860
It's very difficult to know what you feel.

431
00:20:53.860 --> 00:20:56.400
And when we have entire professions like therapists,

432
00:20:56.400 --> 00:20:59.520
who are just trying to get us in touch with our emotions,

433
00:20:59.520 --> 00:21:02.793
it's very difficult to know what I actually want.

434
00:21:04.050 --> 00:21:05.910
And what happens if you have a system

435
00:21:05.910 --> 00:21:09.490
that knows what I want better than me.

436
00:21:09.490 --> 00:21:13.120
So authority gradually shifts to that system.

437
00:21:13.120 --> 00:21:15.830
And what happens if the system can...

438
00:21:15.830 --> 00:21:19.490
If it can predict and understand my feelings so well,

439
00:21:19.490 --> 00:21:22.023
it obviously can manipulate my feelings as well.

440
00:21:23.130 --> 00:21:25.123
The oracle becomes sovereign.

441
00:21:26.230 --> 00:21:28.910
And then authority completely

442
00:21:28.910 --> 00:21:31.710
shifts away from human emotions.

443
00:21:31.710 --> 00:21:35.120
There is a famous Quip about, I think, communism.

444
00:21:35.120 --> 00:21:37.770
That you have a communist before the revolution,

445
00:21:37.770 --> 00:21:39.682
you have a communist agitator

446
00:21:39.682 --> 00:21:43.717
standing in front of a crowd and telling everybody,

447
00:21:43.717 --> 00:21:45.930
"When the revolution comes,

448
00:21:45.930 --> 00:21:48.820
we will all eat strawberries and cream."

449
00:21:48.820 --> 00:21:50.197
And then somebody says,

450
00:21:50.197 --> 00:21:52.840
"But I don't like strawberries and cream."

451
00:21:52.840 --> 00:21:55.650
And then the agitator says, "When the revolution comes,

452
00:21:55.650 --> 00:21:58.640
you will like strawberries and cream."

453
00:21:58.640 --> 00:21:59.940
'Cause you will like what the state

454
00:21:59.940 --> 00:22:00.773
tells you that you like.

455
00:22:00.773 --> 00:22:02.146
Yes.

456
00:22:02.146 --> 00:22:07.146
And in 1917, the way that the state made you like something,

457
00:22:07.540 --> 00:22:10.890
was very crude and not very pleasant.

458
00:22:10.890 --> 00:22:12.130
But in the future,

459
00:22:12.130 --> 00:22:15.370
the state can make you like strawberry and cream

460
00:22:15.370 --> 00:22:19.213
in very sophisticated and pleasant ways.

461
00:22:20.240 --> 00:22:22.573
So where does this lead us?

462
00:22:24.110 --> 00:22:28.810
Specifically in terms of how this is gonna

463
00:22:31.320 --> 00:22:34.750
change market forces and change the political landscape,

464
00:22:34.750 --> 00:22:39.537
and change, specifically how we navigate the world

465
00:22:42.470 --> 00:22:45.370
in terms of careers and making a living.

466
00:22:45.370 --> 00:22:49.410
I mean, you talk a lot about the irrelevance.

467
00:22:49.410 --> 00:22:50.980
Yes.
The irrelevant class.

468
00:22:50.980 --> 00:22:55.980
And in terms of AI and reshaping the discussion not around,

469
00:22:58.020 --> 00:23:00.010
you know, the revolution of the robots

470
00:23:00.010 --> 00:23:02.860
that are coming to kill us, but in the sense that,

471
00:23:02.860 --> 00:23:04.730
it's gonna render a giant swath

472
00:23:04.730 --> 00:23:09.730
of the human population unemployable and irrelevant.

473
00:23:09.960 --> 00:23:14.520
Yeah, there are many, many themes that we can explore.

474
00:23:14.520 --> 00:23:17.450
There is the theme of irrelevance and uselessness that,

475
00:23:17.450 --> 00:23:19.740
again, coming back to the communist revolution,

476
00:23:19.740 --> 00:23:21.130
if in the 20th century,

477
00:23:21.130 --> 00:23:23.770
the big struggle was against exploitation.

478
00:23:23.770 --> 00:23:25.989
They're trying to exploit you.

479
00:23:25.989 --> 00:23:26.920
In the 21st century,

480
00:23:26.920 --> 00:23:29.700
the big struggle might be against irrelevance.

481
00:23:29.700 --> 00:23:31.290
They just don't need you.

482
00:23:31.290 --> 00:23:33.800
And it's much, much more difficult

483
00:23:33.800 --> 00:23:37.143
to struggle against irrelevance than against exploitation.

484
00:23:38.270 --> 00:23:40.270
So this is one thing.

485
00:23:40.270 --> 00:23:42.810
Another thing is that we just don't have any idea

486
00:23:42.810 --> 00:23:44.970
what human life is going to look like,

487
00:23:44.970 --> 00:23:47.260
when more and more of the decisions

488
00:23:47.260 --> 00:23:50.440
are taken by these algorithms on our behalf.

489
00:23:50.440 --> 00:23:52.580
Because for thousands of years,

490
00:23:52.580 --> 00:23:57.430
almost all religious and political and artistic traditions,

491
00:23:58.420 --> 00:24:02.620
depicted life as a drama of decision making,

492
00:24:02.620 --> 00:24:04.770
whether it's a Shakespeare play,

493
00:24:04.770 --> 00:24:07.060
or whether it's a Jane Austin novel,

494
00:24:07.060 --> 00:24:08.840
or whether it's a Hollywood comedy,

495
00:24:08.840 --> 00:24:10.730
or whether it's a theology book,

496
00:24:10.730 --> 00:24:15.430
they all describe life as a kind of journey

497
00:24:15.430 --> 00:24:18.800
that you are walking on the road.

498
00:24:18.800 --> 00:24:22.140
And every few steps, or every few miles,

499
00:24:22.140 --> 00:24:25.550
you reach an intersection and you need to choose,

500
00:24:25.550 --> 00:24:27.940
you have small decisions, what to choose for lunch,

501
00:24:27.940 --> 00:24:32.570
you have big decisions, whom to marry, whom to vote for,

502
00:24:32.570 --> 00:24:35.720
whether to start this career or that career,

503
00:24:35.720 --> 00:24:37.823
whether to go to war or to make peace.

504
00:24:38.720 --> 00:24:42.280
And the whole drama revolves around the decision,

505
00:24:42.280 --> 00:24:44.300
making the right decision.

506
00:24:44.300 --> 00:24:46.190
Now, how does life look like?

507
00:24:46.190 --> 00:24:48.070
How does art look like?

508
00:24:48.070 --> 00:24:50.120
How does theology looks like?

509
00:24:50.120 --> 00:24:52.310
Whenever you reach the intersection,

510
00:24:52.310 --> 00:24:54.747
you just take out the smartphone and you say,

511
00:24:54.747 --> 00:24:56.417
"Okay, Google, what should I do?"

512
00:24:57.295 --> 00:24:58.430
(Yuval and Rich laugh)

513
00:24:58.430 --> 00:25:01.750
Just try, I don't know, "King Lear," (indistinct).

514
00:25:01.750 --> 00:25:02.970
How is...

515
00:25:02.970 --> 00:25:05.930
As somebody who doesn't believe in free will,

516
00:25:05.930 --> 00:25:08.290
how is this different qualitatively?

517
00:25:08.290 --> 00:25:11.810
Oh, I mean, there is a lot of confusion

518
00:25:11.810 --> 00:25:13.810
about free will.

519
00:25:13.810 --> 00:25:15.740
People definitely have a will.

520
00:25:15.740 --> 00:25:18.190
People definitely make decisions all the time.

521
00:25:18.190 --> 00:25:20.080
We make choices all the time.

522
00:25:20.080 --> 00:25:22.440
To say that, "I don't believe in free will,"

523
00:25:22.440 --> 00:25:25.580
and science doesn't even understand what free will means.

524
00:25:25.580 --> 00:25:27.910
It doesn't mean that people don't make decisions.

525
00:25:27.910 --> 00:25:29.870
We make decisions all the time,

526
00:25:29.870 --> 00:25:33.510
but these decisions don't reflect freedom.

527
00:25:33.510 --> 00:25:37.610
They reflect millions of different factors

528
00:25:37.610 --> 00:25:41.460
that we are unaware of the vast majority of them.

529
00:25:41.460 --> 00:25:43.030
My main problem with free will,

530
00:25:43.030 --> 00:25:45.580
aside from the scientific fact that,

531
00:25:45.580 --> 00:25:48.130
it just doesn't make any scientific sense,

532
00:25:48.130 --> 00:25:50.600
is that, if you believe in free will,

533
00:25:50.600 --> 00:25:54.220
you are extremely uncurious about the way

534
00:25:54.220 --> 00:25:57.410
you make decisions about your own desires,

535
00:25:57.410 --> 00:25:58.720
about your own mind.

536
00:25:58.720 --> 00:26:00.550
You just tend to identify

537
00:26:00.550 --> 00:26:02.520
with whatever pops up in your mind.

538
00:26:02.520 --> 00:26:03.827
A desire pops up and,

539
00:26:03.827 --> 00:26:06.280
"Hey, let's me, I chose this,

540
00:26:06.280 --> 00:26:08.290
this is my free will, I'll do it."

541
00:26:08.290 --> 00:26:10.070
On some level though, not to interrupt,

542
00:26:10.070 --> 00:26:14.580
but as a sort of countervailing perspective on this,

543
00:26:14.580 --> 00:26:16.280
presuming that every human being

544
00:26:16.280 --> 00:26:19.300
is the aggregation of their traumas

545
00:26:19.300 --> 00:26:22.120
and their decisions and their various experiences,

546
00:26:22.120 --> 00:26:24.070
packed into that is this idea

547
00:26:24.070 --> 00:26:25.910
that we're not making clear decisions.

548
00:26:25.910 --> 00:26:29.670
Our decisions are clouded by our various experiences

549
00:26:29.670 --> 00:26:31.640
that perhaps prevent us from making

550
00:26:31.640 --> 00:26:33.890
the best decision in our best interest.

551
00:26:33.890 --> 00:26:37.750
So that smartphone might be able to help us

552
00:26:37.750 --> 00:26:41.500
transcend those limitations to make a better decision.

553
00:26:41.500 --> 00:26:42.333
This is why it's so tempting.

554
00:26:42.333 --> 00:26:43.558
Yeah.
I mean,

555
00:26:43.558 --> 00:26:45.840
if the smartphone always made terrible decisions,

556
00:26:45.840 --> 00:26:48.490
then very quickly we would throw it away.

557
00:26:48.490 --> 00:26:51.690
But the thing is, that in more and more fields,

558
00:26:51.690 --> 00:26:54.530
we discover by empirical experience,

559
00:26:54.530 --> 00:26:57.390
that it's a good idea to listen

560
00:26:57.390 --> 00:26:59.970
to Google or Netflix or Amazon.

561
00:26:59.970 --> 00:27:01.170
Like, if you think about something

562
00:27:01.170 --> 00:27:03.720
like navigating your way around New York.

563
00:27:03.720 --> 00:27:06.530
So like I'm now going on this book tour,

564
00:27:06.530 --> 00:27:10.240
so I'm going to here and there, all over the city,

565
00:27:10.240 --> 00:27:12.725
and most of the cab drivers,

566
00:27:12.725 --> 00:27:16.010
they just rely on Google now.
Right.

567
00:27:16.010 --> 00:27:17.380
And it's a good idea.

568
00:27:17.380 --> 00:27:18.970
I mean, you reach an intersection,

569
00:27:18.970 --> 00:27:21.400
your gut feeling tells you, "Turn right."

570
00:27:21.400 --> 00:27:23.780
Google says, "No, no, no, turn left, it's better."

571
00:27:23.780 --> 00:27:26.310
You trust your gut feeling, you're stuck in traffic.

572
00:27:26.310 --> 00:27:27.890
You miss your appointment.

573
00:27:27.890 --> 00:27:30.760
Next time you listen to Google you arrive on time.

574
00:27:30.760 --> 00:27:33.430
So you learn by experience, it's better to listen to them.

575
00:27:33.430 --> 00:27:35.050
They know what they're talking about.

576
00:27:35.050 --> 00:27:37.120
And now it's with traffic,

577
00:27:37.120 --> 00:27:40.370
in 20 years, it'll be decisions like whom to marry,

578
00:27:40.370 --> 00:27:43.190
and what to study in university.

579
00:27:43.190 --> 00:27:44.640
And it will be tempting,

580
00:27:44.640 --> 00:27:47.763
because they will give us very good recommendations.

581
00:27:49.200 --> 00:27:52.690
At the same time, we're moving into this era

582
00:27:52.690 --> 00:27:55.280
where the gestalt of automation

583
00:27:55.280 --> 00:27:57.870
is accelerating at a crazy pace.

584
00:27:57.870 --> 00:28:01.770
And there's a lot of conversations about jobs and lost jobs,

585
00:28:01.770 --> 00:28:05.240
but the problem is much more dire than that.

586
00:28:05.240 --> 00:28:08.124
Can you elaborate a little bit more

587
00:28:08.124 --> 00:28:09.527
on what that landscape is going to look like?

588
00:28:09.527 --> 00:28:10.870
For the job market?
Yeah.

589
00:28:10.870 --> 00:28:15.870
In 2050, and how that impacts generations to come?

590
00:28:16.610 --> 00:28:19.770
Well, the simplistic scenario

591
00:28:19.770 --> 00:28:22.450
is that the robots are coming, they'll take all the jobs,

592
00:28:22.450 --> 00:28:24.050
we'll have nothing to do.

593
00:28:24.050 --> 00:28:27.950
And for some people in some countries, this may be the case.

594
00:28:27.950 --> 00:28:30.880
There are many countries today that rely primarily

595
00:28:30.880 --> 00:28:34.080
on cheap manual labor for the economies,

596
00:28:34.080 --> 00:28:37.420
people working in sweat shops and textile factories.

597
00:28:37.420 --> 00:28:41.023
And these economies may completely crash.

598
00:28:41.860 --> 00:28:44.230
In other countries like in the US,

599
00:28:44.230 --> 00:28:48.810
many jobs will disappear, but many new jobs will appear.

600
00:28:48.810 --> 00:28:50.990
If you bring back textile production

601
00:28:50.990 --> 00:28:53.350
from Honduras to United States,

602
00:28:53.350 --> 00:28:55.500
because you now have 3D printers,

603
00:28:55.500 --> 00:28:58.610
and you now have robots that are far cheaper

604
00:28:58.610 --> 00:28:59.930
than the people in Honduras.

605
00:28:59.930 --> 00:29:03.160
So Honduras is in a very big trouble,

606
00:29:03.160 --> 00:29:05.360
but in the US, you have maybe more jobs,

607
00:29:05.360 --> 00:29:08.030
not in producing textiles, nobody needs that.

608
00:29:08.030 --> 00:29:11.110
But for example, in writing code,

609
00:29:11.110 --> 00:29:13.720
because the main business of the textile industry

610
00:29:13.720 --> 00:29:15.970
is going to be data and code.

611
00:29:15.970 --> 00:29:18.730
You need a lot of data on your customers,

612
00:29:18.730 --> 00:29:22.220
what they want, what is the new fashion,

613
00:29:22.220 --> 00:29:24.370
and about their bodies?

614
00:29:24.370 --> 00:29:27.850
I mean, you can now design a shirt

615
00:29:27.850 --> 00:29:30.870
specifically for your torso.

616
00:29:30.870 --> 00:29:33.010
You don't need to rely on mass production,

617
00:29:33.010 --> 00:29:34.200
like in the Industrial Revolution.

618
00:29:34.200 --> 00:29:36.350
And you'll be able to print that in your home.

619
00:29:36.350 --> 00:29:37.600
And you can print it in your home

620
00:29:37.600 --> 00:29:38.750
or in New Jersey.

621
00:29:38.750 --> 00:29:41.023
You don't have to bring it from Honduras.

622
00:29:42.070 --> 00:29:43.280
But you do need coders,

623
00:29:43.280 --> 00:29:46.020
you do need people who deal with data and so forth.

624
00:29:46.020 --> 00:29:47.930
So there are new jobs.

625
00:29:47.930 --> 00:29:51.260
But the thing is that, these jobs too,

626
00:29:51.260 --> 00:29:54.863
will gradually be automated and change very rapidly.

627
00:29:56.130 --> 00:29:58.860
The landscape in the job market ahead of us,

628
00:29:58.860 --> 00:30:02.950
is going to be extremely hectic and bumpy.

629
00:30:02.950 --> 00:30:07.570
The automation revolution will not be a single big watershed

630
00:30:07.570 --> 00:30:11.740
event that lots of jobs disappear, many new jobs appear.

631
00:30:11.740 --> 00:30:13.590
You have a few years of turbulence,

632
00:30:13.590 --> 00:30:15.020
and then everything settles down

633
00:30:15.020 --> 00:30:17.620
to a new equilibrium, end of story, no.

634
00:30:17.620 --> 00:30:18.960
Like, every 10 years.

635
00:30:18.960 --> 00:30:23.070
I mean, AI is not even near it's full potential.

636
00:30:23.070 --> 00:30:25.400
It's just in its infancy.

637
00:30:25.400 --> 00:30:27.660
We haven't seen anything yet.

638
00:30:27.660 --> 00:30:32.660
So every 10 years you are likely to lose your job

639
00:30:33.000 --> 00:30:35.900
or your job is going to be completely transformed

640
00:30:35.900 --> 00:30:40.900
by the new wave of the latest machine learning wizardry.

641
00:30:41.020 --> 00:30:43.130
And if you want to stay in the game,

642
00:30:43.130 --> 00:30:46.180
you will have to basically reinvent yourself.

643
00:30:46.180 --> 00:30:48.754
And not just once, but repeatedly,

644
00:30:48.754 --> 00:30:53.754
you throw in extended lifespans,

645
00:30:54.330 --> 00:30:59.250
people live longer and they retire at an older age, maybe.

646
00:30:59.250 --> 00:31:02.030
So maybe you need to reinvent yourself

647
00:31:02.030 --> 00:31:05.230
five or six times during your lifetime.

648
00:31:05.230 --> 00:31:07.190
So not just the idea of a job for life,

649
00:31:07.190 --> 00:31:08.530
but a profession for life,

650
00:31:08.530 --> 00:31:11.690
this is going to be completely obsolete.

651
00:31:11.690 --> 00:31:16.293
Yeah. And it's going to require a level of skills

652
00:31:16.293 --> 00:31:21.293
far more advanced than this idea of the coal miner

653
00:31:23.650 --> 00:31:25.410
then becoming a factory worker,

654
00:31:25.410 --> 00:31:27.440
where you can easily translate

655
00:31:27.440 --> 00:31:30.883
some basic skill sets to reeducate people.

656
00:31:31.900 --> 00:31:33.800
It's gonna get a lot more complex.

657
00:31:33.800 --> 00:31:36.090
And you know, even now when you look

658
00:31:36.090 --> 00:31:38.150
at Millennials and Gen Z,

659
00:31:38.150 --> 00:31:41.320
I mean, they're very acclimated to this freelance economy.

660
00:31:41.320 --> 00:31:43.140
Very few of them look at a job

661
00:31:43.140 --> 00:31:46.080
as something they're gonna do for the rest of their life.

662
00:31:46.080 --> 00:31:49.020
But I think that's only going to accelerate,

663
00:31:49.020 --> 00:31:50.390
the lifespan on those careers

664
00:31:50.390 --> 00:31:51.703
are gonna get shorter and shorter.

665
00:31:51.703 --> 00:31:54.720
Then the thing is that they are young now.

666
00:31:54.720 --> 00:31:56.890
When you are 25, when you're 30,

667
00:31:56.890 --> 00:31:59.750
reinventing yourself is difficult.

668
00:31:59.750 --> 00:32:01.970
It's never easy to reinvent yourself,

669
00:32:01.970 --> 00:32:04.580
but it's still feasible.

670
00:32:04.580 --> 00:32:08.070
But when you get to the age of say,

671
00:32:08.070 --> 00:32:12.023
40, 50, 60, it becomes more and more difficult.

672
00:32:13.360 --> 00:32:18.360
And this is what awaits all of us.

673
00:32:18.620 --> 00:32:21.370
And certainly, the younger generation.

674
00:32:21.370 --> 00:32:25.080
So that the fact that they may enjoy to some extent

675
00:32:25.080 --> 00:32:27.950
the gig economy in their 20s,

676
00:32:27.950 --> 00:32:30.970
this is not necessarily what you would like your life

677
00:32:30.970 --> 00:32:33.290
to be in your 50s.
Right.

678
00:32:33.290 --> 00:32:35.350
But maybe you don't have any option.

679
00:32:35.350 --> 00:32:37.250
Maybe if you want to stay in the game,

680
00:32:37.250 --> 00:32:39.440
you need to run faster and faster.

681
00:32:39.440 --> 00:32:42.842
And the greatest difficulty in this respect

682
00:32:42.842 --> 00:32:45.680
is likely to be psychological.

683
00:32:45.680 --> 00:32:50.680
That, how do you maintain the kind of mental flexibility

684
00:32:50.760 --> 00:32:54.630
that maybe at age 20 comes natural to you?

685
00:32:54.630 --> 00:32:59.110
But for most people is not natural at all at age 50 or 60.

686
00:32:59.110 --> 00:33:00.249
Right.

687
00:33:00.249 --> 00:33:02.390
So resilience and emotional intelligence

688
00:33:02.390 --> 00:33:07.390
become paramount in a way that we haven't seen before,

689
00:33:08.440 --> 00:33:12.554
which calls into question our entire educational system.

690
00:33:12.554 --> 00:33:14.023
Yes, exactly.

691
00:33:14.930 --> 00:33:16.880
We're still stuck in this,

692
00:33:16.880 --> 00:33:21.753
you know, sort of Elizabethan, Victorian model,

693
00:33:21.753 --> 00:33:24.380
a big model of how we're educating children,

694
00:33:24.380 --> 00:33:26.290
that's very information based,

695
00:33:26.290 --> 00:33:29.620
and is just out of step with the times

696
00:33:29.620 --> 00:33:32.440
and what is going to be required.

697
00:33:32.440 --> 00:33:36.880
So how do we rethink how we're educating our youth?

698
00:33:36.880 --> 00:33:38.890
You know, everybody...

699
00:33:38.890 --> 00:33:43.070
Almost everybody, I guess, who deals with these issues

700
00:33:43.070 --> 00:33:47.070
know that the educational system is bankrupt.

701
00:33:47.070 --> 00:33:50.830
It's not adapted to the realities of the 21st century,

702
00:33:50.830 --> 00:33:53.290
but we don't have an alternative model.

703
00:33:53.290 --> 00:33:56.590
We have a lot of experiments, but so far,

704
00:33:56.590 --> 00:34:00.010
none of these experiments seems to be scalable.

705
00:34:00.010 --> 00:34:02.200
You can have a small experimental school,

706
00:34:02.200 --> 00:34:04.617
which does amazing things, but then you think,

707
00:34:04.617 --> 00:34:08.070
"Okay, now, how do I scale this to millions of teachers

708
00:34:08.070 --> 00:34:11.130
and tens of millions of pupils?"

709
00:34:11.130 --> 00:34:14.120
That's a big challenge.

710
00:34:14.120 --> 00:34:18.170
And I certainly don't have the answers.

711
00:34:18.170 --> 00:34:23.170
I would definitely agree that the things we need to invest

712
00:34:23.440 --> 00:34:26.740
in education are emotional intelligence

713
00:34:26.740 --> 00:34:30.680
and mental flexibility, and learning how to learn,

714
00:34:30.680 --> 00:34:32.780
rather than cramming information.

715
00:34:32.780 --> 00:34:34.760
That's the easy part to say that,

716
00:34:34.760 --> 00:34:36.900
but how do you teach emotional intelligence?

717
00:34:36.900 --> 00:34:38.798
Yeah. I mean, if you were raising a child,

718
00:34:38.798 --> 00:34:41.253
what is the curriculum that you would create?

719
00:34:44.510 --> 00:34:46.483
That's...

720
00:34:46.483 --> 00:34:48.540
Come on, you gotta have the answers.

721
00:34:48.540 --> 00:34:50.430
I don't know.
I know it's tough, right?

722
00:34:50.430 --> 00:34:51.263
That's...

723
00:34:51.263 --> 00:34:55.550
Again, and also, I can come up with this list

724
00:34:55.550 --> 00:34:57.927
of things we can do, but then you think,

725
00:34:57.927 --> 00:34:59.010
"Okay, but we can...

726
00:34:59.010 --> 00:35:01.210
We need to do it in a systematic way

727
00:35:01.210 --> 00:35:03.280
with hundreds of thousands of teachers,

728
00:35:03.280 --> 00:35:06.430
weigh supervisors with budgets for the government

729
00:35:06.430 --> 00:35:10.800
who want to know that the money is not wasted on whatever."

730
00:35:10.800 --> 00:35:14.840
And then you realize, "Okay, so my idea of,

731
00:35:14.840 --> 00:35:16.440
let's take a group of kids to the woods

732
00:35:16.440 --> 00:35:17.580
and live there for a week,

733
00:35:17.580 --> 00:35:21.260
and build a house in the tree and things like..."

734
00:35:21.260 --> 00:35:25.410
Okay, how do I do it with government budgets

735
00:35:25.410 --> 00:35:28.120
and millions of kids and so forth?

736
00:35:28.120 --> 00:35:31.070
That's much more difficult than I thought at first.

737
00:35:31.070 --> 00:35:33.170
And that's the big problem,

738
00:35:33.170 --> 00:35:37.590
it's not with coming up with a solution

739
00:35:37.590 --> 00:35:41.670
for a small number of kids in an affluent suburb.

740
00:35:41.670 --> 00:35:43.780
It's coming up with a solution,

741
00:35:43.780 --> 00:35:45.990
which is scalable to the whole country,

742
00:35:45.990 --> 00:35:48.010
and basically to the whole world.

743
00:35:48.010 --> 00:35:50.870
I mean, the worst problems, as we mentioned earlier,

744
00:35:50.870 --> 00:35:53.700
may not even be in the United States.

745
00:35:53.700 --> 00:35:55.700
There are far more likely to be in places

746
00:35:55.700 --> 00:35:58.630
like Honduras or like Brazil, or like Bangladesh.

747
00:35:58.630 --> 00:36:00.320
Yeah. The places that are most prone

748
00:36:00.320 --> 00:36:02.080
to be automated first.

749
00:36:02.080 --> 00:36:06.400
Yeah, it's the places that it's easiest

750
00:36:06.400 --> 00:36:08.760
to automate what they do.

751
00:36:08.760 --> 00:36:12.870
And the profits from the automation will not go to Honduras,

752
00:36:12.870 --> 00:36:14.293
they will go to California.

753
00:36:16.060 --> 00:36:19.590
So, if coal miners in Pennsylvania lose their jobs,

754
00:36:19.590 --> 00:36:24.250
you can envision a situation in which the government taxes,

755
00:36:24.250 --> 00:36:26.770
the big high tech companies in Silicon Valley,

756
00:36:26.770 --> 00:36:29.500
and in order to help the coal miners

757
00:36:29.500 --> 00:36:30.780
in Pennsylvania.
Right.

758
00:36:30.780 --> 00:36:33.270
But I don't see the government of the US

759
00:36:33.270 --> 00:36:35.730
taxing Google and Amazon in the US

760
00:36:35.730 --> 00:36:38.160
to help people in Honduras.

761
00:36:38.160 --> 00:36:38.993
Why do you think

762
00:36:38.993 --> 00:36:41.706
we're not talking about this enough?

763
00:36:41.706 --> 00:36:46.310
I mean, we talk a lot about jobs and job loss,

764
00:36:46.310 --> 00:36:50.910
but that conversation is very rooted in the present moment.

765
00:36:50.910 --> 00:36:53.090
There's not a lot of deep thinking,

766
00:36:53.090 --> 00:36:57.250
going into forecasting what the future might hold.

767
00:36:57.250 --> 00:37:00.060
And perhaps that's systemic.

768
00:37:00.060 --> 00:37:05.060
That's a fundamental flaw in our liberal democratic system

769
00:37:05.770 --> 00:37:08.760
that relies upon economic market forces

770
00:37:08.760 --> 00:37:10.930
to solve these problems for us.

771
00:37:10.930 --> 00:37:14.280
And doesn't provide the bandwidth

772
00:37:14.280 --> 00:37:17.030
or the incentive to think long term

773
00:37:17.030 --> 00:37:18.710
about what the world may look like,

774
00:37:18.710 --> 00:37:22.860
so that we can plan for it in the present.

775
00:37:22.860 --> 00:37:25.040
Well, it's always difficult

776
00:37:25.040 --> 00:37:27.709
to deal with future problems,

777
00:37:27.709 --> 00:37:29.700
when you have more urgent business,

778
00:37:29.700 --> 00:37:31.490
maybe less important business,

779
00:37:31.490 --> 00:37:33.880
but more urgent business right now.

780
00:37:33.880 --> 00:37:35.550
And this is especially true in a system

781
00:37:35.550 --> 00:37:38.287
when you have the four year election cycle that,

782
00:37:38.287 --> 00:37:41.167
"Okay, why should I now spend my time

783
00:37:41.167 --> 00:37:45.160
and my political capital on solving a problem

784
00:37:45.160 --> 00:37:47.990
which will hit us in 20 years?

785
00:37:47.990 --> 00:37:51.890
That won't get me reelected in four years."

786
00:37:51.890 --> 00:37:56.750
And that's one very important ingredient of the problem,

787
00:37:56.750 --> 00:37:59.330
but on a more basic level, it's just, you know,

788
00:37:59.330 --> 00:38:01.273
everything is just very, very new.

789
00:38:02.620 --> 00:38:06.250
Five years ago, all this talk about AI

790
00:38:06.250 --> 00:38:08.560
still sounded like science fiction.

791
00:38:08.560 --> 00:38:11.940
The attention we now have on AI...

792
00:38:11.940 --> 00:38:14.940
You know, it happened so fast.

793
00:38:14.940 --> 00:38:18.410
I remember when I published my first book, "Sapiens,"

794
00:38:18.410 --> 00:38:23.220
it was 2014, just four years ago.

795
00:38:23.220 --> 00:38:26.285
And the book doesn't mention AI at all,

796
00:38:26.285 --> 00:38:27.680
as far as I can remember.

797
00:38:27.680 --> 00:38:28.920
Nobody was talking about it.

798
00:38:28.920 --> 00:38:31.670
I mean, you know, a few people in laboratories,

799
00:38:31.670 --> 00:38:33.580
in Silicon Valley on some universities,

800
00:38:33.580 --> 00:38:36.840
they realized, "Oh, it's something big is in the pipe."

801
00:38:36.840 --> 00:38:41.840
But in 2013, 2014, AI was still not in the news.

802
00:38:43.290 --> 00:38:44.950
Now it's everywhere.

803
00:38:44.950 --> 00:38:48.070
So four years is not such a long time to...

804
00:38:48.070 --> 00:38:49.030
Right.

805
00:38:49.030 --> 00:38:52.060
To kind of start a completely

806
00:38:52.060 --> 00:38:54.890
new political debate.

807
00:38:54.890 --> 00:38:56.380
I think humans are hardwired

808
00:38:56.380 --> 00:39:01.380
to kind of fundamentally view the world as somewhat static.

809
00:39:01.970 --> 00:39:05.180
Liberal democracy is here, it's always gonna be here.

810
00:39:05.180 --> 00:39:07.300
It's hard for us to imagine a future

811
00:39:07.300 --> 00:39:10.290
that looks qualitatively different

812
00:39:10.290 --> 00:39:13.260
from the one that we live in now.

813
00:39:13.260 --> 00:39:18.080
And how do you square that?

814
00:39:18.080 --> 00:39:20.270
Like, squaring that with the rapidity...

815
00:39:20.270 --> 00:39:22.390
I mean, we've never seen the rapidity of change

816
00:39:22.390 --> 00:39:23.610
that we're seeing now.

817
00:39:23.610 --> 00:39:27.740
And it's just continuing to accelerate and it's bewildering,

818
00:39:27.740 --> 00:39:30.430
we're playing catch up in a way that,

819
00:39:30.430 --> 00:39:33.560
if we're not careful is gonna lead

820
00:39:33.560 --> 00:39:36.660
to dire circumstances for all of us.

821
00:39:36.660 --> 00:39:40.750
And so I feel like we almost need a different system,

822
00:39:40.750 --> 00:39:44.490
a system of star chambers of really smart people

823
00:39:44.490 --> 00:39:47.670
who are getting together to talk about these ideas,

824
00:39:47.670 --> 00:39:50.860
to prophylactically, come up with solutions

825
00:39:50.860 --> 00:39:53.880
that will keep us on a positive trajectory

826
00:39:53.880 --> 00:39:56.083
and not lead us astray.

827
00:39:57.940 --> 00:40:00.110
You know, this is allegedly

828
00:40:01.150 --> 00:40:03.300
what they are trying to do in China that...

829
00:40:04.900 --> 00:40:07.670
Now, forget elections, forget legal democracy,

830
00:40:07.670 --> 00:40:10.180
we need a very different kind of systems,

831
00:40:10.180 --> 00:40:14.820
a system that can look ahead decades and plan things

832
00:40:14.820 --> 00:40:19.750
without all this troublesome stuff of elections.

833
00:40:19.750 --> 00:40:21.510
And it has its advantages,

834
00:40:21.510 --> 00:40:25.640
and it has some very big disadvantages as well.

835
00:40:25.640 --> 00:40:29.650
And as much as I have a lot of faith

836
00:40:29.650 --> 00:40:34.650
in experts and in scientists, there are dangers in giving,

837
00:40:37.160 --> 00:40:40.142
like, all the authority or too much authority

838
00:40:40.142 --> 00:40:43.210
to a small group of experts.

839
00:40:43.210 --> 00:40:46.957
And thinking that, "Well, they'll figure out what to do."

840
00:40:48.140 --> 00:40:52.320
Especially as these experts are in the end, still humans,

841
00:40:52.320 --> 00:40:57.320
with all the biases and all the flows of human nature.

842
00:40:58.060 --> 00:41:00.367
Again, then the next temptation is say,

843
00:41:00.367 --> 00:41:02.590
"Okay, so let's take it out of the hands of humans

844
00:41:02.590 --> 00:41:04.470
and give it to algorithms."

845
00:41:04.470 --> 00:41:07.320
But then you have entirely new problems.

846
00:41:07.320 --> 00:41:10.380
Part of them are still an inheritance for the human problems

847
00:41:10.380 --> 00:41:12.940
because the humans design the algorithms.

848
00:41:12.940 --> 00:41:15.300
And very often they design the algorithms

849
00:41:15.300 --> 00:41:17.360
with their own biases built in,

850
00:41:17.360 --> 00:41:19.093
and they don't even realize it,

851
00:41:20.090 --> 00:41:22.500
but they're also entirely new problems.

852
00:41:22.500 --> 00:41:26.930
When the decision making process

853
00:41:26.930 --> 00:41:30.560
becomes completely opaque to humans and this,

854
00:41:30.560 --> 00:41:31.770
whether we like it or not,

855
00:41:31.770 --> 00:41:35.810
whether it is this solution or a solution or whatever,

856
00:41:35.810 --> 00:41:37.830
this is going to happen more and more.

857
00:41:37.830 --> 00:41:40.090
That as the world becomes more complicated,

858
00:41:40.090 --> 00:41:42.730
as the pace of change increases,

859
00:41:42.730 --> 00:41:45.170
most people will just not be able to understand

860
00:41:45.170 --> 00:41:47.160
what the hell is happening.

861
00:41:47.160 --> 00:41:49.770
And more and more power will be concentrated

862
00:41:49.770 --> 00:41:53.740
in non-human hands, in the hands of these algorithms.

863
00:41:53.740 --> 00:41:55.920
And we see it happening for example right now,

864
00:41:55.920 --> 00:41:58.970
with the global financial markets,

865
00:41:58.970 --> 00:42:02.130
that so much of the transactions there,

866
00:42:02.130 --> 00:42:04.020
are being done by algorithms.

867
00:42:04.020 --> 00:42:06.460
And very often even the best human experts

868
00:42:06.460 --> 00:42:08.530
can't explain what is happening.

869
00:42:08.530 --> 00:42:10.233
Like with the flash crashes.

870
00:42:12.020 --> 00:42:14.200
And this is just likely to increase.

871
00:42:14.200 --> 00:42:16.970
Even if you keep a human at the top,

872
00:42:16.970 --> 00:42:19.210
like the CEO is a human being,

873
00:42:19.210 --> 00:42:22.110
the president, we want a human president,

874
00:42:22.110 --> 00:42:25.740
but as everybody who worked at least for some time

875
00:42:25.740 --> 00:42:28.310
in a big bureaucratic system knows,

876
00:42:28.310 --> 00:42:30.670
very often, the person at the head,

877
00:42:30.670 --> 00:42:32.930
he or she, they are just figureheads.

878
00:42:32.930 --> 00:42:35.650
The real decisions are taken down the line

879
00:42:35.650 --> 00:42:38.410
by the people who prepare the different options.

880
00:42:38.410 --> 00:42:40.687
Like, you come to the president with three options,

881
00:42:40.687 --> 00:42:42.590
"This is what we can do with Iran,"

882
00:42:42.590 --> 00:42:47.590
or, "This is what we can do with the crisis in the market."

883
00:42:48.860 --> 00:42:50.670
And okay, so the president gets to pick

884
00:42:50.670 --> 00:42:52.180
between three options,

885
00:42:52.180 --> 00:42:55.610
but who decided that these are the three options?

886
00:42:55.610 --> 00:42:57.080
Why only these three?

887
00:42:57.080 --> 00:42:58.590
What about the other five options

888
00:42:58.590 --> 00:43:00.920
which never were never brought to the table?

889
00:43:00.920 --> 00:43:04.527
In many, many cases, the real decision is,

890
00:43:04.527 --> 00:43:07.520
"Which three options to bring to the president?"

891
00:43:07.520 --> 00:43:10.930
Very often two options are just so unthinkable,

892
00:43:10.930 --> 00:43:13.230
that you know what he's going to do anyway.

893
00:43:13.230 --> 00:43:15.860
And what are the motivations behind the choices

894
00:43:15.860 --> 00:43:18.770
made to present those three options?

895
00:43:18.770 --> 00:43:19.950
Everybody has their own agenda.

896
00:43:19.950 --> 00:43:20.783
Exactly.

897
00:43:20.783 --> 00:43:21.940
And now the question is,

898
00:43:21.940 --> 00:43:24.420
what happens when the three choices

899
00:43:24.420 --> 00:43:28.060
which are presented to the president or the CEO,

900
00:43:28.060 --> 00:43:31.350
they were shaped by algorithms,

901
00:43:31.350 --> 00:43:34.620
according to the way algorithms understand the world.

902
00:43:34.620 --> 00:43:39.560
And it's so complicated that we can't understand

903
00:43:39.560 --> 00:43:41.817
why the algorithm decided that,

904
00:43:41.817 --> 00:43:45.910
"These are the three options, that we need to choose from."

905
00:43:45.910 --> 00:43:47.810
And this is going to be...

906
00:43:47.810 --> 00:43:50.050
We are going to encounter this problem

907
00:43:50.050 --> 00:43:52.290
in more and more areas in life.

908
00:43:52.290 --> 00:43:54.990
Like, yeah, it starts with the simple, stupid things.

909
00:43:54.990 --> 00:43:57.380
Like, why does Netflix give me

910
00:43:57.380 --> 00:44:01.580
these five options for the next movie?

911
00:44:01.580 --> 00:44:05.200
Why these five movies? Why not 50 others?

912
00:44:05.200 --> 00:44:07.230
But here, you know, the stakes are very low.

913
00:44:07.230 --> 00:44:09.900
So, okay, so I watched a bad movie,

914
00:44:09.900 --> 00:44:13.260
but what happens when you have an incredibly

915
00:44:14.200 --> 00:44:16.220
sophisticated financial system

916
00:44:16.220 --> 00:44:18.720
that no human being can understand,

917
00:44:18.720 --> 00:44:21.677
and then the computer alerts the president that,

918
00:44:21.677 --> 00:44:24.380
"Look, there is now a crisis in the markets,

919
00:44:24.380 --> 00:44:27.180
and these are the three options you can do."

920
00:44:27.180 --> 00:44:29.920
And he doesn't even understand what they mean.

921
00:44:29.920 --> 00:44:31.557
And he certainly doesn't understand,

922
00:44:31.557 --> 00:44:33.670
"Why do you think there is a crisis?

923
00:44:33.670 --> 00:44:36.647
And why do you think that these are the three options?"

924
00:44:36.647 --> 00:44:37.760
"Well, you are human,

925
00:44:37.760 --> 00:44:41.660
you'll never understand it, but trust us, we know."

926
00:44:41.660 --> 00:44:45.160
And it doesn't matter if you call it a democracy

927
00:44:45.160 --> 00:44:47.690
or a dictatorship or whatever.

928
00:44:47.690 --> 00:44:51.533
It's a kind of system that we have never encountered before.

929
00:44:55.970 --> 00:44:58.323
I don't know what to make of all this.

930
00:44:58.323 --> 00:44:59.374
(Yuval and Rich laugh)

931
00:44:59.374 --> 00:45:02.170
You know?
That's a human reaction.

932
00:45:02.170 --> 00:45:04.289
Yeah, it's like..

933
00:45:04.289 --> 00:45:07.080
What do you even do with that?

934
00:45:07.080 --> 00:45:08.540
How do we even process that?

935
00:45:08.540 --> 00:45:10.280
How do we find a way forward?

936
00:45:10.280 --> 00:45:12.240
Oh, you can actually look at the present.

937
00:45:12.240 --> 00:45:16.810
I mean, how many people today, let's say in the US,

938
00:45:16.810 --> 00:45:19.390
can honestly say they understand

939
00:45:19.390 --> 00:45:21.343
how the financial system works?

940
00:45:22.790 --> 00:45:23.790
There's probably a lot of people

941
00:45:23.790 --> 00:45:25.760
who will claim to understand it,

942
00:45:25.760 --> 00:45:28.140
whether they truly do or not.

943
00:45:28.140 --> 00:45:30.150
Even, let's be very generous,

944
00:45:30.150 --> 00:45:31.287
just the people who claimed,

945
00:45:31.287 --> 00:45:34.130
"I really understand the financial system,"

946
00:45:34.130 --> 00:45:35.473
out of how many people out there in the US,

947
00:45:35.473 --> 00:45:38.170
like 300 million, 350 million?

948
00:45:38.170 --> 00:45:40.917
How many would honestly, like say,

949
00:45:40.917 --> 00:45:44.300
"Yes, I understand how this system works."

950
00:45:44.300 --> 00:45:46.540
I would imagine very, very few.

951
00:45:46.540 --> 00:45:49.470
I mean, maybe a million out of 350 million.

952
00:45:49.470 --> 00:45:52.990
That's still a very small percentage.

953
00:45:52.990 --> 00:45:54.890
And we are already living in a world,

954
00:45:54.890 --> 00:45:58.570
which is so complicated that,

955
00:45:58.570 --> 00:46:02.700
most people don't understand how something like that...

956
00:46:02.700 --> 00:46:06.690
Something important like the financial system works.

957
00:46:06.690 --> 00:46:10.890
And one of the things that is happening in politics is that,

958
00:46:10.890 --> 00:46:13.680
people talk about the things they understand,

959
00:46:13.680 --> 00:46:16.890
not about the things that are important.

960
00:46:16.890 --> 00:46:18.490
And it happens in the political...

961
00:46:18.490 --> 00:46:19.837
I mean, I understand immigration.

962
00:46:19.837 --> 00:46:21.920
"Okay, you have these foreigners, they want to come here,

963
00:46:21.920 --> 00:46:23.377
I don't like it, okay."

964
00:46:24.350 --> 00:46:26.330
I think one of the main attractions

965
00:46:26.330 --> 00:46:29.707
of talking about immigration is that you understand,

966
00:46:29.707 --> 00:46:30.770
or at least you think you understand

967
00:46:30.770 --> 00:46:31.830
what you're talking about.

968
00:46:31.830 --> 00:46:36.193
If we are going to debate the interest rates

969
00:46:36.193 --> 00:46:38.640
that the federal reserve sets,

970
00:46:38.640 --> 00:46:40.330
I don't understand how to talk about it.

971
00:46:40.330 --> 00:46:42.987
Well, and the people that would purport

972
00:46:42.987 --> 00:46:45.230
to understand it disagree amongst themselves.

973
00:46:45.230 --> 00:46:47.547
And fundamentally, all of this is based on,

974
00:46:47.547 --> 00:46:50.350
"This is something you talk about all the time,"

975
00:46:50.350 --> 00:46:52.470
fundamentally, it's based on a story

976
00:46:52.470 --> 00:46:57.370
that we all agree is fact, but which is really myth.

977
00:46:57.370 --> 00:46:59.930
Yes. But you know, in the case of immigration,

978
00:46:59.930 --> 00:47:02.190
at least we know what the story is.

979
00:47:02.190 --> 00:47:05.910
In the case of the interest rates of the federal reserve,

980
00:47:05.910 --> 00:47:09.080
most people just don't understand the story at all.

981
00:47:09.080 --> 00:47:11.200
So you can't have...

982
00:47:11.200 --> 00:47:12.520
I mean, there is nothing...

983
00:47:12.520 --> 00:47:14.020
People like to disagree.

984
00:47:14.020 --> 00:47:15.617
They like to shout and argue,

985
00:47:15.617 --> 00:47:17.720
"And I think this, and you are wrong,"

986
00:47:17.720 --> 00:47:20.020
but you can't have this kind of argument

987
00:47:20.020 --> 00:47:21.140
about the interest rates,

988
00:47:21.140 --> 00:47:23.350
because you don't understand anything.

989
00:47:23.350 --> 00:47:25.180
So you talk about immigration.

990
00:47:25.180 --> 00:47:28.240
You know, it's like in businesses,

991
00:47:28.240 --> 00:47:33.240
like, you have a meeting of the staff,

992
00:47:34.000 --> 00:47:36.470
and you have two items on the agenda.

993
00:47:36.470 --> 00:47:40.440
One item is which pension fund,

994
00:47:40.440 --> 00:47:45.440
which pension program to have for the staff,

995
00:47:45.590 --> 00:47:48.960
and the other item is which coffee machine to buy.

996
00:47:48.960 --> 00:47:51.700
So we would have a five minutes debate

997
00:47:51.700 --> 00:47:54.487
about a pension fund because nobody understands,

998
00:47:54.487 --> 00:47:56.710
"Okay, let's just do whatever he says.

999
00:47:56.710 --> 00:47:59.200
I mean, we trust the chief financial officer

1000
00:47:59.200 --> 00:48:00.620
that he knows what he's doing."

1001
00:48:00.620 --> 00:48:02.610
And then you have a two hours argument...

1002
00:48:02.610 --> 00:48:05.230
About the coffee,
About the coffee machine.

1003
00:48:05.230 --> 00:48:06.783
Because this is, we understand.

1004
00:48:06.783 --> 00:48:08.690
I mean, we can talk.

1005
00:48:08.690 --> 00:48:10.190
The beauty of humans.

1006
00:48:11.430 --> 00:48:12.570
Shifting gears a little bit,

1007
00:48:12.570 --> 00:48:16.160
I wanna talk about global climate change.

1008
00:48:16.160 --> 00:48:19.180
And how you think about this problem

1009
00:48:19.180 --> 00:48:24.180
and where we're at in terms of our ability to reckon with,

1010
00:48:25.240 --> 00:48:28.440
one of the enumerated things that you discuss

1011
00:48:28.440 --> 00:48:31.943
in your new book, the greatest challenges facing humankind.

1012
00:48:33.550 --> 00:48:35.900
Well, climate change,

1013
00:48:35.900 --> 00:48:39.020
the two most important things to realize about it is that,

1014
00:48:39.020 --> 00:48:42.250
it's happening, it's not some future scenario.

1015
00:48:42.250 --> 00:48:43.860
It's not like, you know,

1016
00:48:43.860 --> 00:48:47.290
nuclear war is an existential threat to humankind,

1017
00:48:47.290 --> 00:48:49.530
but it's just a future possibility,

1018
00:48:49.530 --> 00:48:51.510
it's not happening right now.

1019
00:48:51.510 --> 00:48:54.360
Climate change is happening right now.

1020
00:48:54.360 --> 00:48:57.380
So we need to do something.

1021
00:48:57.380 --> 00:49:00.050
And the second thing we need to realize is that,

1022
00:49:00.050 --> 00:49:02.910
the only effective way to counter climate change

1023
00:49:02.910 --> 00:49:05.680
is through global corporation.

1024
00:49:05.680 --> 00:49:08.280
It should be absolutely obvious that,

1025
00:49:08.280 --> 00:49:11.800
you cannot stop climate change on the national level.

1026
00:49:11.800 --> 00:49:13.830
It's not a national problem.

1027
00:49:13.830 --> 00:49:17.710
No country is ecologically independent,

1028
00:49:17.710 --> 00:49:20.550
even the most powerful countries like China or the US,

1029
00:49:20.550 --> 00:49:22.860
they are not independent countries

1030
00:49:22.860 --> 00:49:24.383
when it comes to the climate.

1031
00:49:25.420 --> 00:49:26.253
And this is something

1032
00:49:26.253 --> 00:49:29.490
which is very annoying and threatening,

1033
00:49:29.490 --> 00:49:33.240
especially for people from the nationalist right.

1034
00:49:33.240 --> 00:49:35.623
Which is...
We're in this breakdown

1035
00:49:35.623 --> 00:49:38.930
of global cooperation at the moment

1036
00:49:38.930 --> 00:49:40.480
with the rise of nationalism,

1037
00:49:40.480 --> 00:49:42.880
which is not helping this problem.

1038
00:49:42.880 --> 00:49:45.900
Yeah. It's just taking us in the wrong direction.

1039
00:49:45.900 --> 00:49:48.520
And I don't think it's therefore a coincidence

1040
00:49:48.520 --> 00:49:50.660
that almost all the people who deny

1041
00:49:50.660 --> 00:49:52.027
the reality of climate change,

1042
00:49:52.027 --> 00:49:54.710
they come from the nationalist right.

1043
00:49:54.710 --> 00:49:55.840
And at first thought you,

1044
00:49:55.840 --> 00:49:59.300
it seems like a very surprising coincidence,

1045
00:49:59.300 --> 00:50:02.680
why is it that almost all the climate change deniers

1046
00:50:02.680 --> 00:50:04.100
are right-wing nationalists?

1047
00:50:04.100 --> 00:50:06.530
Why don't you have like left-wing socialists

1048
00:50:06.530 --> 00:50:08.390
who deny climate change?

1049
00:50:08.390 --> 00:50:11.360
And historically speaking, it's even more surprising,

1050
00:50:11.360 --> 00:50:15.210
because historically caring about the environment

1051
00:50:15.210 --> 00:50:18.470
was something which characterized the right

1052
00:50:18.470 --> 00:50:20.780
and which characterized nationalists.

1053
00:50:20.780 --> 00:50:22.960
They are the ones who care about forests

1054
00:50:22.960 --> 00:50:25.920
and going out to nature and connecting to the earth.

1055
00:50:25.920 --> 00:50:27.750
Whereas the left-wing socialists,

1056
00:50:27.750 --> 00:50:29.590
they love industry and big cities

1057
00:50:29.590 --> 00:50:31.930
and the proletariat and things like that.

1058
00:50:31.930 --> 00:50:35.350
And when you look in the first half of the 20th century,

1059
00:50:35.350 --> 00:50:37.410
so environmental 'causes,

1060
00:50:37.410 --> 00:50:40.313
these are the kinds of things that Nazis cared about.

1061
00:50:41.410 --> 00:50:44.490
And suddenly, this becomes a left wing thing.

1062
00:50:44.490 --> 00:50:46.810
And the conservatives,

1063
00:50:46.810 --> 00:50:49.720
they don't care about conserving the environment.

1064
00:50:49.720 --> 00:50:54.720
And my explanation for what happened is the realization

1065
00:50:54.800 --> 00:50:58.330
that you just can't do much on the national level.

1066
00:50:58.330 --> 00:51:03.330
So if your ideal is to be this kind of isolated fortress,

1067
00:51:03.810 --> 00:51:05.640
we don't need global corporation,

1068
00:51:05.640 --> 00:51:07.090
we don't like globalization,

1069
00:51:07.090 --> 00:51:09.956
we don't like to cooperate with foreigners.

1070
00:51:09.956 --> 00:51:14.956
So we don't like to admit that there are global problems

1071
00:51:15.360 --> 00:51:17.960
that demand global corporation,

1072
00:51:17.960 --> 00:51:21.930
and which just cannot be solved on a national basis.

1073
00:51:21.930 --> 00:51:24.330
Yeah. It's distinct from the problem

1074
00:51:24.330 --> 00:51:26.700
presented by nuclear proliferation.

1075
00:51:26.700 --> 00:51:30.720
Because on some level, nationalism still provides

1076
00:51:32.450 --> 00:51:35.530
nation states with the ability to prevent

1077
00:51:35.530 --> 00:51:38.000
that Armageddon event from happening,

1078
00:51:38.000 --> 00:51:40.180
because of mutual incentivization.

1079
00:51:40.180 --> 00:51:41.013
Yeah.

1080
00:51:41.013 --> 00:51:43.580
Because of mutual destruction that would occur.

1081
00:51:43.580 --> 00:51:46.350
But that sensibility does not apply

1082
00:51:46.350 --> 00:51:49.110
to addressing the climate change problem.

1083
00:51:49.110 --> 00:51:51.390
Yeah, for many countries,

1084
00:51:51.390 --> 00:51:55.010
if you're a small country, you are completely helpless.

1085
00:51:55.010 --> 00:51:57.110
You think about the extreme case,

1086
00:51:57.110 --> 00:52:01.220
if you are a small island nation in the Pacific Ocean,

1087
00:52:01.220 --> 00:52:04.700
like Kiribati, which is one of the...

1088
00:52:04.700 --> 00:52:07.590
In danger of being swallowed by the ocean,

1089
00:52:07.590 --> 00:52:09.750
if climate change continues,

1090
00:52:09.750 --> 00:52:12.570
there is nothing you can do about to save yourself.

1091
00:52:12.570 --> 00:52:15.470
I mean, you can reduce greenhouse

1092
00:52:15.470 --> 00:52:18.110
gas emissions in Kiribati to zero.

1093
00:52:18.110 --> 00:52:21.470
You can even start absorbing CO2 from the atmosphere.

1094
00:52:21.470 --> 00:52:22.620
It won't help you.

1095
00:52:22.620 --> 00:52:25.380
If you don't get the Chinese and the Indians

1096
00:52:25.380 --> 00:52:27.490
and the Americans and the Europeans

1097
00:52:27.490 --> 00:52:31.273
to adopt better environmental policies, it won't help you.

1098
00:52:32.750 --> 00:52:35.890
And even a country like China,

1099
00:52:35.890 --> 00:52:40.780
that now realizes the danger it faces from climate change.

1100
00:52:40.780 --> 00:52:44.970
You cannot save Shanghai from the Pacific Ocean,

1101
00:52:44.970 --> 00:52:48.280
unless you cooperate with the Germans

1102
00:52:48.280 --> 00:52:50.370
and the Americans and the Russians.

1103
00:52:50.370 --> 00:52:53.830
And what makes it even more problematic is that,

1104
00:52:53.830 --> 00:52:57.230
some nations might calculate,

1105
00:52:57.230 --> 00:52:59.920
and perhaps even correctly, to a certain degree,

1106
00:52:59.920 --> 00:53:03.963
that they actually stand to benefit from climate change.

1107
00:53:05.330 --> 00:53:08.390
This is particularly true of Russia,

1108
00:53:08.390 --> 00:53:12.240
which could benefit quite a lot from climate change,

1109
00:53:12.240 --> 00:53:13.950
from global warming.

1110
00:53:13.950 --> 00:53:17.390
And it's also true of many of the countries...

1111
00:53:17.390 --> 00:53:18.870
In the short term.

1112
00:53:18.870 --> 00:53:21.170
At least in the short term, yes.

1113
00:53:21.170 --> 00:53:23.050
But they could...

1114
00:53:23.050 --> 00:53:25.890
They have very few assets on the ocean.

1115
00:53:25.890 --> 00:53:29.690
So if the oceans rise, it's not a problem,

1116
00:53:29.690 --> 00:53:31.180
a big problem for Russia.

1117
00:53:31.180 --> 00:53:36.180
If Siberia warms up and becomes a bread basket of the world,

1118
00:53:36.580 --> 00:53:39.820
that's a plus, if the Arctic Ocean melts...

1119
00:53:39.820 --> 00:53:42.950
Do you think those conversations are going on?

1120
00:53:42.950 --> 00:53:44.990
I don't know, but could be.

1121
00:53:44.990 --> 00:53:49.670
Again, another big plus, if the Arctic Ocean melts,

1122
00:53:49.670 --> 00:53:52.760
then all their shipping from China to Europe,

1123
00:53:52.760 --> 00:53:55.380
instead of going through Singapore

1124
00:53:55.380 --> 00:53:58.570
and the long way through Suez or the Cape of Good hope,

1125
00:53:58.570 --> 00:54:00.400
you can just reroute them,

1126
00:54:00.400 --> 00:54:03.260
through Kamchatka and Novaya Zemlya.

1127
00:54:03.260 --> 00:54:05.800
And suddenly Kamchatka is the new Singapore.

1128
00:54:05.800 --> 00:54:07.690
So that's a big plus.

1129
00:54:07.690 --> 00:54:12.270
And also Russia and also Iran and Qatar and Saudi Arabia,

1130
00:54:12.270 --> 00:54:15.750
their economy is based on fossil fuels.

1131
00:54:15.750 --> 00:54:20.750
So if we suddenly have a big shift from burning fossil fuels

1132
00:54:20.930 --> 00:54:24.590
to renewable energy, to wind, solar, nuclear,

1133
00:54:24.590 --> 00:54:29.590
that's going to create an enormous economic shock

1134
00:54:30.250 --> 00:54:33.643
for a country like Qatar, or like Russia,

1135
00:54:35.027 --> 00:54:37.503
whose economy depends on oil and gas.

1136
00:54:38.380 --> 00:54:42.770
So for this reason too, it's not going to be so easy.

1137
00:54:42.770 --> 00:54:45.690
Yeah. So what is the bridge forward

1138
00:54:45.690 --> 00:54:50.500
towards global cooperation around this issue?

1139
00:54:50.500 --> 00:54:52.270
Human beings as storytellers?

1140
00:54:52.270 --> 00:54:57.070
What is this story that we can craft and all agree upon,

1141
00:54:57.070 --> 00:55:00.160
to begin to address this in a constructive way?

1142
00:55:00.160 --> 00:55:01.640
Well, there are three things, first of all,

1143
00:55:01.640 --> 00:55:04.810
to realize that for at least the vast majority of humankind,

1144
00:55:04.810 --> 00:55:06.740
this is a terrible danger.

1145
00:55:06.740 --> 00:55:08.950
To unite people, you need an enemy,

1146
00:55:08.950 --> 00:55:11.490
but the enemy doesn't have to be another human.

1147
00:55:11.490 --> 00:55:15.530
The enemy can be a danger that threatens all of us

1148
00:55:15.530 --> 00:55:18.330
and climate change definitely fits the bill.

1149
00:55:18.330 --> 00:55:22.350
So you have an enemy, which is always good in a story.

1150
00:55:22.350 --> 00:55:24.590
But it doesn't have a face.
Yes, it doesn't.

1151
00:55:24.590 --> 00:55:27.010
It's ephemeral.
Yes.

1152
00:55:27.010 --> 00:55:31.040
It's not as good an enemy as Hitler or as Voldemort,

1153
00:55:31.040 --> 00:55:32.023
but it's something.

1154
00:55:32.883 --> 00:55:34.333
I mean, you can work with it.

1155
00:55:35.690 --> 00:55:40.690
Secondly, besides an enemy, you need to have hope

1156
00:55:41.400 --> 00:55:43.360
that we can do something about it,

1157
00:55:43.360 --> 00:55:46.050
and not only prevent the worst outcome,

1158
00:55:46.050 --> 00:55:49.190
but we can actually benefit from it in many ways.

1159
00:55:49.190 --> 00:55:51.970
Over the last few years, there is a growing realization

1160
00:55:51.970 --> 00:55:55.050
that climate change is not just a danger,

1161
00:55:55.050 --> 00:55:56.860
that we need, like good pollutants

1162
00:55:56.860 --> 00:56:01.860
to consume less and drive less and spend less

1163
00:56:02.050 --> 00:56:05.710
in order to save the planet, we can actually benefit a lot

1164
00:56:05.710 --> 00:56:08.730
from developing new eco-friendly technologies.

1165
00:56:08.730 --> 00:56:12.860
Even economically, there is a lot of room for if...

1166
00:56:12.860 --> 00:56:16.140
One thing that you can't do in today's world

1167
00:56:16.140 --> 00:56:18.630
is to stop economic growth,

1168
00:56:18.630 --> 00:56:20.150
because this is the number one value,

1169
00:56:20.150 --> 00:56:21.230
whether we like it or not,

1170
00:56:21.230 --> 00:56:23.800
this is the number one value of almost all countries.

1171
00:56:23.800 --> 00:56:25.470
They can say whatever they want,

1172
00:56:25.470 --> 00:56:28.770
they can call themselves communist or liberal democracies

1173
00:56:28.770 --> 00:56:32.390
or Hindu or Muslim or Jewish or secular,

1174
00:56:32.390 --> 00:56:35.320
their number one value is really economic growth.

1175
00:56:35.320 --> 00:56:38.160
So if you need, in order to stop climate change,

1176
00:56:38.160 --> 00:56:40.130
to stop economic growth, it won't happen.

1177
00:56:40.130 --> 00:56:44.436
No, it has to be incentivized

1178
00:56:44.436 --> 00:56:46.150
in a capitalistic way.

1179
00:56:46.150 --> 00:56:47.170
It's in the best interest

1180
00:56:47.170 --> 00:56:48.910
of all of these corporations to go green.

1181
00:56:48.910 --> 00:56:50.820
Yeah, but not only incentivized

1182
00:56:50.820 --> 00:56:53.500
in the sense that we have more regulations,

1183
00:56:53.500 --> 00:56:55.520
and we take externalities into account,

1184
00:56:55.520 --> 00:56:58.520
but actually developing some types

1185
00:56:58.520 --> 00:57:00.690
of new eco-friendly technologies

1186
00:57:00.690 --> 00:57:03.363
is going to be really beneficial.

1187
00:57:04.720 --> 00:57:06.733
And maybe to give one example,

1188
00:57:07.670 --> 00:57:11.760
which is close to my heart is clean meat,

1189
00:57:11.760 --> 00:57:14.540
that one of the main polluters

1190
00:57:14.540 --> 00:57:16.940
and one of the main 'causes for climate change

1191
00:57:16.940 --> 00:57:19.610
and for other ecological problems around the world

1192
00:57:19.610 --> 00:57:21.773
is the dairy and meat industry.

1193
00:57:22.700 --> 00:57:26.100
And it also has the added problem

1194
00:57:26.100 --> 00:57:28.230
of 'causing enormous suffering

1195
00:57:28.230 --> 00:57:30.880
to billions of sentient beings.

1196
00:57:30.880 --> 00:57:35.390
Now, we can develop, and we are developing

1197
00:57:35.390 --> 00:57:39.670
new technologies of producing milk and dairy,

1198
00:57:39.670 --> 00:57:42.220
simply from cells.

1199
00:57:42.220 --> 00:57:43.690
If you want a steak,

1200
00:57:43.690 --> 00:57:46.230
you don't need to raise an entire cow

1201
00:57:46.230 --> 00:57:47.570
and then slaughter the cow.

1202
00:57:47.570 --> 00:57:49.700
You just grow a steak.

1203
00:57:49.700 --> 00:57:52.920
Now, again, 10 years ago, this sounded like science fiction,

1204
00:57:52.920 --> 00:57:57.380
but this is now being done by quite a number of startups

1205
00:57:57.380 --> 00:57:59.550
and companies all over the world.

1206
00:57:59.550 --> 00:58:02.350
The initial price I think for a hamburger

1207
00:58:02.350 --> 00:58:05.810
was $300,000 in 2013.

1208
00:58:05.810 --> 00:58:08.550
And now I heard it's down to about $10

1209
00:58:08.550 --> 00:58:10.110
and it's still going down.

1210
00:58:10.110 --> 00:58:14.930
So in five or 10 years, you can have a cheaper hamburger,

1211
00:58:14.930 --> 00:58:16.690
a much more ethical hamburger,

1212
00:58:16.690 --> 00:58:18.830
and also a much healthier hamburger,

1213
00:58:18.830 --> 00:58:19.990
because if you raise it...

1214
00:58:19.990 --> 00:58:21.450
If you don't raise a cow,

1215
00:58:21.450 --> 00:58:24.420
if you just grow the hamburger from cells,

1216
00:58:24.420 --> 00:58:29.420
you can determine the exact ingredients,

1217
00:58:29.620 --> 00:58:32.660
and you don't need so much antibiotics

1218
00:58:32.660 --> 00:58:34.250
and you don't need all the fat.

1219
00:58:34.250 --> 00:58:37.450
And you can have a much, much, not just cheaper and ethical,

1220
00:58:37.450 --> 00:58:39.090
but much healthier hamburger.

1221
00:58:39.090 --> 00:58:41.317
So we don't need to think about it as,

1222
00:58:41.317 --> 00:58:43.320
"Okay, we have to do some difficult stuff

1223
00:58:43.320 --> 00:58:44.620
in order to prevent climate change."

1224
00:58:44.620 --> 00:58:47.750
There are actually a lot of potential benefits there.

1225
00:58:47.750 --> 00:58:51.710
Yeah, the clean meat revolution is happening.

1226
00:58:51.710 --> 00:58:54.820
It's unclear what the timeframe is.

1227
00:58:54.820 --> 00:58:57.500
Might take a little bit longer than we may realize,

1228
00:58:57.500 --> 00:58:59.570
but it's certainly coming,

1229
00:58:59.570 --> 00:59:03.270
in the same way that automated driving is coming.

1230
00:59:03.270 --> 00:59:06.100
There might be a weird acclimation period

1231
00:59:06.100 --> 00:59:08.710
of people trying to get used to this idea.

1232
00:59:08.710 --> 00:59:09.930
But I think that they will.

1233
00:59:09.930 --> 00:59:10.900
And you know, this is a...

1234
00:59:10.900 --> 00:59:12.910
I've explored this topic on the podcast.

1235
00:59:12.910 --> 00:59:14.810
I had Paul Shapiro on who wrote "Clean Meat,"

1236
00:59:14.810 --> 00:59:16.740
who I know you know, you wrote the forward to his book.

1237
00:59:16.740 --> 00:59:19.060
And I've had Bruce Friedrich on.

1238
00:59:19.060 --> 00:59:21.190
I'm trying to get from the Good Food Institute.

1239
00:59:21.190 --> 00:59:25.160
You know him? Amazing individual.

1240
00:59:25.160 --> 00:59:28.140
I'm trying to get Uma Valeti on from Memphis Meats,

1241
00:59:28.140 --> 00:59:30.490
'cause I think it's fascinating what's happening there.

1242
00:59:30.490 --> 00:59:34.060
And we have a dire crisis in the form

1243
00:59:34.060 --> 00:59:35.400
of factory farming right now,

1244
00:59:35.400 --> 00:59:38.840
that's reeking ecological havoc on the world.

1245
00:59:38.840 --> 00:59:41.320
We need a more innovative, better way,

1246
00:59:41.320 --> 00:59:43.820
to feed our growing population,

1247
00:59:43.820 --> 00:59:48.462
which brings me to the subject of veganism.

1248
00:59:48.462 --> 00:59:51.360
You're a vegan yourself.

1249
00:59:51.360 --> 00:59:53.210
You've done a million interviews, it doesn't get...

1250
00:59:53.210 --> 00:59:54.740
People don't ask you that much about this,

1251
00:59:54.740 --> 00:59:59.120
but I'm interested in what motivated you

1252
00:59:59.120 --> 01:00:01.010
to adopt this lifestyle?

1253
01:00:01.010 --> 01:00:01.843
Well, first I would say

1254
01:00:01.843 --> 01:00:03.950
that I'm vegan-ish.
Vegan-ish, okay.

1255
01:00:03.950 --> 01:00:06.350
Yeah. I'm not very religious about it.

1256
01:00:07.290 --> 01:00:10.167
I try to limit my involvement as far as possible,

1257
01:00:10.167 --> 01:00:12.690
'cause the meat and dairy industry,

1258
01:00:12.690 --> 01:00:17.480
but I don't see it as a kind of purity laws.

1259
01:00:17.480 --> 01:00:19.240
Like, I come from a Jewish background.

1260
01:00:19.240 --> 01:00:22.390
So I know all about the OCDs that people can develop

1261
01:00:22.390 --> 01:00:26.300
about ritualistic, purity laws with food.

1262
01:00:26.300 --> 01:00:27.793
So I try not to go there.

1263
01:00:28.900 --> 01:00:33.613
The reason that brought me to veganism is purely ethical.

1264
01:00:34.840 --> 01:00:39.340
Just a growing awareness of the enormous suffering

1265
01:00:39.340 --> 01:00:43.580
we are inflicting on billions of sentient beings,

1266
01:00:43.580 --> 01:00:48.580
just to indulge our culinary fences.

1267
01:00:49.610 --> 01:00:52.060
I mean, at least in the 21st century,

1268
01:00:52.060 --> 01:00:56.330
if you were an Inuit, living a thousands years ago,

1269
01:00:56.330 --> 01:00:58.610
and the only thing you have to eat around you

1270
01:00:58.610 --> 01:01:01.713
is seals and fish, then, okay.

1271
01:01:02.560 --> 01:01:05.330
I won't try to convince you to become vegan,

1272
01:01:05.330 --> 01:01:07.979
but if you live in New York in 2018,

1273
01:01:07.979 --> 01:01:10.653
that defense doesn't hold.
Right.

1274
01:01:11.870 --> 01:01:15.820
And so I think that just for the ethical reasons,

1275
01:01:15.820 --> 01:01:18.823
this is what we need to do.
Yeah.

1276
01:01:20.260 --> 01:01:23.590
Our hour is almost up and I wanna be conscious of your time,

1277
01:01:23.590 --> 01:01:26.520
but I wanna end this conversation,

1278
01:01:26.520 --> 01:01:28.260
bringing it back to where we started,

1279
01:01:28.260 --> 01:01:32.210
which is this idea of clarity as power.

1280
01:01:32.210 --> 01:01:37.210
And your methodology for being as clear as possible

1281
01:01:38.530 --> 01:01:41.390
is your devotion to your meditation practice,

1282
01:01:41.390 --> 01:01:42.660
for posing and meditation.

1283
01:01:42.660 --> 01:01:45.260
You meditate two hours a day.

1284
01:01:45.260 --> 01:01:48.090
So perhaps a good way to end this is to talk a little bit

1285
01:01:48.090 --> 01:01:50.970
about the impact that that practice

1286
01:01:50.970 --> 01:01:54.810
has had on you as a human being

1287
01:01:54.810 --> 01:01:57.063
and how it informs the work that you do.

1288
01:01:58.830 --> 01:02:00.540
I don't think I could have survived

1289
01:02:00.540 --> 01:02:03.193
these last few years without the meditation.

1290
01:02:04.490 --> 01:02:06.490
You know, all the publication of the book

1291
01:02:06.490 --> 01:02:09.623
and all the attention and traveling around the world,

1292
01:02:10.750 --> 01:02:14.330
without a peace of mind that the meditation brings,

1293
01:02:14.330 --> 01:02:15.400
I could not have done it.

1294
01:02:15.400 --> 01:02:17.700
I couldn't have written the book in the first place

1295
01:02:17.700 --> 01:02:19.320
without the meditation.

1296
01:02:19.320 --> 01:02:24.140
Because as you say, it brings a kind of clarity and focus,

1297
01:02:24.140 --> 01:02:26.131
especially if you try to condense

1298
01:02:26.131 --> 01:02:30.935
the entire history of the world into like 450 pages,

1299
01:02:30.935 --> 01:02:31.820
which is very ambitious.

1300
01:02:31.820 --> 01:02:33.080
Yes, you need...

1301
01:02:33.080 --> 01:02:35.510
It gave you this incredible objectivity though,

1302
01:02:35.510 --> 01:02:38.163
to see everything from 10,000 feet.

1303
01:02:39.150 --> 01:02:40.140
Yeah.

1304
01:02:40.140 --> 01:02:42.740
But you need to be able to focus,

1305
01:02:42.740 --> 01:02:44.730
because there are so many details

1306
01:02:44.730 --> 01:02:46.320
that can take you here and there.

1307
01:02:46.320 --> 01:02:49.923
And then you end up writing 4,000 pages and not 400 pages.

1308
01:02:50.970 --> 01:02:55.720
But really for me, the most important contribution

1309
01:02:55.720 --> 01:03:00.720
of meditation is to really be able to see reality as it is,

1310
01:03:00.760 --> 01:03:05.610
and to tell the difference between what is really happening

1311
01:03:05.610 --> 01:03:10.530
and what is just stories generated by the mind,

1312
01:03:10.530 --> 01:03:12.039
the human mind...

1313
01:03:12.039 --> 01:03:13.000
You could pull your smartphone out

1314
01:03:13.000 --> 01:03:14.860
and the algorithm would tell you.

1315
01:03:14.860 --> 01:03:16.960
This is the one thing that so far,

1316
01:03:16.960 --> 01:03:19.653
algorithms are not getting even close to telling you.

1317
01:03:22.380 --> 01:03:27.060
The human mind is a factory for generating fictional stories

1318
01:03:27.060 --> 01:03:30.060
about myself, about my family,

1319
01:03:30.060 --> 01:03:32.730
about my country, about the world.

1320
01:03:32.730 --> 01:03:35.470
And it's so difficult to tell the difference

1321
01:03:35.470 --> 01:03:39.850
between the stories we invent and objective reality.

1322
01:03:39.850 --> 01:03:42.320
And when I came to my first meditation

1323
01:03:42.320 --> 01:03:45.363
with the person I reiterated, was 18 years ago?

1324
01:03:46.430 --> 01:03:48.460
I was doing a PhD in Oxford.

1325
01:03:48.460 --> 01:03:50.820
And I thought I was a very smart person.

1326
01:03:50.820 --> 01:03:52.930
And that I know myself very well,

1327
01:03:52.930 --> 01:03:55.550
and I'm my in control of my life.

1328
01:03:55.550 --> 01:04:00.020
And the teacher gave us the first few days,

1329
01:04:00.020 --> 01:04:03.910
the practice is very, very simple in a way,

1330
01:04:03.910 --> 01:04:05.550
just observe your breath,

1331
01:04:05.550 --> 01:04:08.120
just observe when the breath is coming in,

1332
01:04:08.120 --> 01:04:10.800
be aware when it's coming in, you know it's coming in,

1333
01:04:10.800 --> 01:04:13.150
when it's going out, you just know it's going out.

1334
01:04:13.150 --> 01:04:16.150
You don't need to do anything, not a breathing exercise.

1335
01:04:16.150 --> 01:04:17.540
You don't need to control the breath.

1336
01:04:17.540 --> 01:04:19.870
You just know it's coming in.

1337
01:04:19.870 --> 01:04:22.500
It sounds like the simplest thing in the world.

1338
01:04:22.500 --> 01:04:25.290
And I was absolutely shocked

1339
01:04:25.290 --> 01:04:28.350
that I couldn't do it for more than 10 seconds.

1340
01:04:28.350 --> 01:04:31.490
Like, I would try to just know, "Oh, it's coming in or out?"

1341
01:04:31.490 --> 01:04:33.460
And within like, five seconds,

1342
01:04:33.460 --> 01:04:37.087
the mind would run away to some memory, some fantasy,

1343
01:04:37.087 --> 01:04:38.830
"Oh, I forgot I needed to do this,

1344
01:04:38.830 --> 01:04:41.190
so I needed to do that, and whatever."

1345
01:04:41.190 --> 01:04:45.890
And I realize, I know almost nothing about my mind.

1346
01:04:45.890 --> 01:04:49.010
I have absolutely no control over it.

1347
01:04:49.010 --> 01:04:51.200
We need to start from zero.

1348
01:04:51.200 --> 01:04:53.520
I mean, and this was an...

1349
01:04:53.520 --> 01:04:55.220
I think it was the most important thing

1350
01:04:55.220 --> 01:04:57.053
anybody ever told me in my life.

1351
01:04:58.030 --> 01:05:02.200
And it was the most shocking realization of my life.

1352
01:05:02.200 --> 01:05:06.240
And also...
And transformative.

1353
01:05:06.240 --> 01:05:09.080
Yeah, very, very transformative.

1354
01:05:09.080 --> 01:05:13.630
And from then on, both in my private life,

1355
01:05:13.630 --> 01:05:16.320
and also in my work,

1356
01:05:16.320 --> 01:05:21.320
I try to just stick with this very simple exercise.

1357
01:05:22.630 --> 01:05:25.180
Just try to see what is really happening right now.

1358
01:05:25.180 --> 01:05:29.510
Yeah. And you go on annual, extended retreats,

1359
01:05:29.510 --> 01:05:32.860
30 to 60 days, yeah.
Yeah, I've been...

1360
01:05:32.860 --> 01:05:34.240
Last year, it was 60 days,

1361
01:05:34.240 --> 01:05:36.198
the year before that it was 45 days.

1362
01:05:36.198 --> 01:05:38.130
And silent the entire time?
Silent, yeah.

1363
01:05:38.130 --> 01:05:39.280
Absolute silence.

1364
01:05:39.280 --> 01:05:42.030
Definitely no smartphone and computers,

1365
01:05:42.030 --> 01:05:44.020
but also you don't talk with anybody there.

1366
01:05:44.020 --> 01:05:48.752
You're just you're with your mind 60 days.

1367
01:05:48.752 --> 01:05:52.510
(Yuval and Rich laugh)

1368
01:05:52.510 --> 01:05:53.610
Unbelievable.

1369
01:05:54.761 --> 01:05:57.010
Our time is up, thank you so much.

1370
01:05:57.010 --> 01:05:58.203
I really appreciate.

1371
01:05:59.110 --> 01:06:00.873
I appreciate the work that you do.

1372
01:06:02.010 --> 01:06:04.160
I wish you well, best of luck with the new book,

1373
01:06:04.160 --> 01:06:06.147
everybody should pick up the new book,

1374
01:06:06.147 --> 01:06:08.610
"21 Lessons for the 21st Century."

1375
01:06:08.610 --> 01:06:11.300
And thank you very much, Yuval.

1376
01:06:11.300 --> 01:06:13.590
Thank you for listening.
Yeah. How do you feel?

1377
01:06:13.590 --> 01:06:14.423
Oh, I feel great.
Good.

1378
01:06:14.423 --> 01:06:15.950
It's fun.
All right. Awesome.

1379
01:06:15.950 --> 01:06:19.659
Thanks, you guys, peace, relax.

1380
01:06:19.659 --> 01:06:22.076
(soft music)

